28-year-old coke found dead in San Francisco shopping mall
The 28-year-old chef, who recently moved to San Francisco, was found dead on the staircase of a local shopping center this week.
The victim's brother says he didn't know of anyone who wanted to harm his brother. "He'd finally got his business in order."
The body, found on Wednesday morning, was identified as 28-year-old Frank Galicia of San Francisco, the San Francisco Forensic Office said.
According to the San Francisco Police Department, the death is being investigated as a homicide.
The victim's brother Louis Galicia told ABC's KGO radio station in San Francisco that Frank, who had previously worked as a chef in Boston, had found his dream job when he became the chef of San Francisco's Sons & Daughters restaurant six months ago.
A spokesman for the Sons & Daughters restaurant said the death was a shock to all of them.
"We're a small and close-knit team, like a family, and it's a big deal," the spokesperson says.
"Our thoughts are with Frank's family and his friends."
Louis Galicia says Frank first lived in hostels, but he had finally got his affairs in order.
"He found the apartment, and he had a girlfriend," Louis Galicia told the KGO station.
Louis Galicia says he didn't know anyone who wanted to harm his little brother.
"He was kind and big-hearted.
His way of being together with the family was cooking for all of us," says Louis Galicia.
"He never wanted to get into a fight with anyone.
He was the brother who went with the river.
Despite all the injustices in the world, he was an unpolished diamond that shone every day," he says.
Everyone who knows anything about the incident will be asked to call the San Francisco Police Department's hotline 415-575-4444.
Struggling junior doctors call for new industrial action
Representatives of junior doctors in the United Kingdom (mainly specialised doctors in Finland) have invoked their trade union and requested permission for further industrial action in the dispute over a new employment contract.
The Junior Medical Committee (JDC), which is part of the UK's Pharmaceutical Trade Union (BMA), is requesting support from its entire government for further industrial action from the beginning of September.
According to the JDC, the ministers have not solved the problems with the agreement.
Junior physicians and medical students rejected the agreement with the BMA in a vote in July.
58% of the members who took the vote rejected the agreement.
Ellen McCourt, chairman of the Junior Medical Committee, posted on Twitter a letter from the JDC on Twitter saying that the board "stubbornly silenced" on issues that McCourt said led to the rejection of the agreement.
The letter reads: "In the light of this, the JDC's Board of Directors has, in its vote, rejected a completely new treaty proposal and insists that new formal consultations on all our concerns take place.
In response to the government's silence, JDC's Board of Directors has today submitted an official request to the BMA's Board of Directors requesting permission for gradually advancing expansive industrial action from the beginning of September."
Due to controversy, junior doctors have already been on strike six times this year, which included the first complete work stoppages ever seen in the history of Britain's public health care system.
Johann Malawana, the head of BMA's junior doctors, resigned from his position after the vote, as he did not accept the negotiated terms and conditions recommended by the BMA.
Malawana told BMA members at pre-voting meetings that the agreement was good and should be approved, with 54 000 junior physicians and medical students taking part in the vote.
After the vote, Health Minister Jeremy Hunt said the agreement would come into force and apply to English doctors.
Russia and Turkey: a coalition of outsiders?
It was a gesture that ended the crisis.
The leaders of Russia and Turkey met on Tuesday to treasulate on reconciliation and to finalise an eight-month war of words and economic sanctions.
However, when Vladimir Putin received his Turkish counterpart in the golden hall of his palace in St. Peter's Palace, I got a clear impression that Ankara had a greater desire for reconciliation.
The parties shook hands,
but Putin's smile looked skinny and he didn't get much warmth, even by his own restrained standard.
Recep Tayyip Erdogan, on the other hand, repeatedly talked about his ‘good friend’ Mr. Putin – five times, according to a report.
I didn't stick to the calculations myself.
In addition, he promised that relations with Russia would recover even better than before the crisis.
The next day, a newspaper in our country wrote that Erdogan acted as if nothing bad had ever happened.
Personally, I understood his enthusiasm quite the opposite.
But the constant coolness that exudes from Putin showed that the head of Russia has not forgotten anything.
In fact, the first thing Putin mentioned in his opening speech was the cause of the crisis: Turkey shot down a Russian fighting plane on the Syrian border.
Moscow's reaction at the time of the incident was furious.
Putin attacked Ankara with strict words and accused him of stabbing in the back.
The offense was even greater because it came from an supposed friend.
Gaining true confidence is going to be difficult, maybe even impossible.
The public opinion of the Russian people has also changed since November.
For months, state-controlled medies ran a thorough and aggressive campaign against Ankara.
However, it suddenly seemed that the Turks were the reason for everything.
Erdogan's own family has benefited from illegal oil trafficking in ISIS-controlled areas of Syria.
Erdogan has strongly denied the accusations.
However, the official message from St. Petersburg was that it was time to move forward.
At the end of the day, this meeting was held only so that Putin received the requested apology from President Erdogan.
In this way, Russia could get some kind of victory.
The benefits of declaring truce for Ankara are clear.
Above all, Erdogan needs all possible friends after he was almost ousted last month in a failed coup.
Recurring terrorist attacks in Turkey have also clearly shaken him.
There is also an economic motive for reconciliation.
Russia's sanctions have hit Turker hard, especially the charter flight ban, as several million Russian tourists normally travel to the Turkish coast every year.
The number has collapsed by almost 90%.
As far as Russia is concerned, tour operators and charter companies will certainly breathe a sigh of relief when air traffic can eventually be resumed.
They're putting their faith in a late-sesson rush into the Mediterranean.
And again this week, state television has predicted that the price of fruit and vegetables will fall, as imports of agricultural products from Turkey will be allowed again.
"Tourists there, tomatoes back here," the economic magazine Vedemost said in an article.
But the visit also had a greater political value for Moscow.
Ankara is angry with the West, because it thinks the West reacted sluggishly to the coup attempt.
Without forgetting Turkey's EU membership negotiations and Putin's interference, Putin, who is eager to take advantage of the cool relations and undermine Turkey's ties to the West.
Russia's leader undoubtedly won bonus points from Ankara when it expressed its support to the elected authorities after the coup attempt.
This was self-evident to Moscow, which has its own concern about the transition of power.
Thus, Russia and Turkey could convey the image described by an "alignment of outsiders" at its summit in a flashy seaside palace: two countries that feel they have been rejected and mistreated by the West unite their forces.
Despite public reconciliation, there are still great disagreements between the two.
The most significant is Syria, in which Moscow has created a picture of itself as a peacebuilder, but in which Russia and Turkey support the opposings.
It says a lot that, after nearly three hours of initial negotiations, both presidents told a press conference that they had not dealt with the subject at all.
The Turkish president deliberately declined to answer a question about the disagreements between the two countries, while Putin emphasized them.
There is no clear consensus between the two countries on where they could seek consensus in Syria.
In view of the possibility of a complete catastrophe caused by the NATO member of Turkey when it fired down a Russian fighting plane, it is undoubtedly better that, after months of open hostility, these two heads of state are once again even on speaking terms.
Royal Bank of Scotcot banks are disappearing from customers outside of Scotland
According to the President of RBS Bank, the bank's operations outside Scotland will be reduced in such a way that in the future it will only offer business services, i.e. so-called back office services.
The Royal Bank of Scotland disappears from its customers outside of Scotland.
Ross McEwan told BBC Scotland that the RBS brand was originally associated with the bank's global ambitions.
The bank had to back down from its ambitions when it almost collapsed eight years ago and had to be rescued.
During that time, brand strategists have used RBS to protect other consumer finance brands.
It sponds millions of pounds of international sports, such as the Six Nations rugby and Wimbledon champion Andy Murray.
But now it's been considered right to allow more national brands into the foreground.
Royal Bank of Scotland will remain available to Scottish customers, but RBS will not be used.
In England and Wales, all RBS names will be changed to NatWest, with the exception of the head office and stock exchange listing.
Ulster Bank brand is already in use in the Republic of Ireland and Northern Ireland.
In addition, there are other smaller private banking brands that will gain greater weight, such as Coutts, Adam & Co, Drummond and Holt's Military Bank.
McEwan was interviewed during a presentation for customers and staff in Inverness-shire.
He told BBC Scotland: "RBS is transforming into an investment brand and because that's what our staff employs because we're now transforming much more into a bank of different brands.
As the bank itself became a global brand, RBS also became a global brand.
Now our aspirations are no longer global, but local.
Each of these brands represents something quite different in their own community, and our staff works with our customers under these brands."
RBS has already announced that it will stop sponsoring the Six Nations rugby, and it has raised the profile of different brands in sports sponsorship.
"Now is the right time to become a banket with different brands, because it's fundamentally a question of how we can make the bank better for our customers," says the bank's president.
"Three years ago, it would have been very scornful if we had said that we will be an excellent bank for our customers and adopt brands.
But the work we do and the focus on the needs of our customers instead of our own needs have changed a lot.
We can bring those brands back again, so I think that's the right time."
Frankie Dettori rode Predilection to victory in Newmarket and achieved his 3,000th victory
Detter sprays champagne after winning twice in a row at the Newmarket, which meant his 3,000th victory.
Jockey Frankie Dettori secured his 3,0000c victory in the British championship at Newmarket on Friday.
He reached this milestone by riding his horse Predilection twice to victory on the same night on the track of his locality. Predilection is a horse trained by John Gosden.
The Italian Dettori, 45, is the sixth jockey to reach this milestone on a steady track in the running races.
Earliers to him, the same has been achieved by Sir Gordon Richards, Doug Smith, Lester Piggott, Pat Eddery and Willie Carson.
The three-time master jockey Dettor, who previously won in Ghayyari, says: "This is special.
My feelings are on the surface because my family's here too.
I'm very happy that this happened in Newmarket, because that's where I ended up 30 years ago.
The event is not a big one, not a Royal Ascot, but an ordinary Friday night and an audience of 20 000 people.
“I’m relieved and I’ve felt great humility.”
Dettori receives cheers with Predilection after their victory
Two jockeys have reached a milestone in steeplechase races, and they are champion jockey Richard Johnson and retired 20-time champion Sir Anthony McCoy, who won more than 4,300 times in his record-breaking career.
Lester Piggott, nine-time Derby winner, 4,493 wins
"Frankie is one of the best jockeys of this time, and it's no surprise that it's exactly he's reached this milestone that few people have reached.
He's elegant and strong, but his tactical superiority is what distinguishes him from others.
He is a fantasical representative of racing, his enthusiasm and pruning has brought supporters to the sport for years."
"A few weeks ago, I told Frankie at Epsom that he would've already won 6 000 races if he didn't only compete on weekends and only in big races!
Seriously, Frankie's riding career in the UK has been victorious for almost 30 years, and it's been great to watch.
He's a lively personality, which is good for British racing, but above all, he's an incredible jockey."
Frankie Dettori ventilated his achievement in an emblem-liked way by jumping vigorously off his horse.
"From flying entry to high-flight landings, Frankie Dettori has been a prime example of British racing for two decades.
There's been room for ups and downs.
He has won numerous major races, including his famous victory in Ascot in 1996 "Magnificent Seven", but in addition, he has survived a plane crash and was banned from competition due to drug abuse.
When the ban ended three years ago, he initially had trouble starting, but he showed an admirable strength of character, showed his skeptics and returned to his best.
And he won't be quitting his career for a long time – Dettori has recently said that he wants to continue his racing career at least until he's 50."
Putin fires regime chief Sergei Ivanov
Russia's president, Vladimir Putin, unexpectedly dismissed his regime chief Sergei Ivanov.
Ivanov has been part of Putin's trusted inner circle for several years.
Ivanov, 63, was now appointed Special Representative for the Environment and Transport.
According to the Kremlin, President Putin "decided to relieve Ivanov of his duties as the head of Russia's presidential administration," but no justification was given.
Anton Vaino, who has served as Ivanov's deputy manager since 2012, was appointed.
Persecution, 44, is a former diplomat.
Born in Tallinn in 1992, Vaino graduated from the prestigious Moscow National Institute of International Relations (MGIMO) and has previously worked at the Embassy in Tokyo.
He later led the presidential protocol department and the government's office, according to the Kremlin's Russian-language website.
Vaino thanked Putin for his appointment in the following words: "I am grateful for your confidence.
In my opinion, the most important task of the administration is to support your actions as head of state, which means making draft laws and supervising how to implement the instructions you provide."
Putin told the Russian broadcaster on Friday that Ivanov himself had asked for resignation and recommended Persecution as his successor.
Are the smiles real?
This transfer has caused confusion in Moscow.
Sergei Ivanov has long belonged to Vladimir Putin's close circle, and, like Putin, he also has a KGB past.
As administrator, he was one of the most influential men in the country.
At a meeting with President Putin on Russian state television, Ivanov receded at his own request.
Despite the smiles showned to the cameras, the explanations convince only a few, especially in the run-up to the parliamentary elections.
Is the firing the result of some kind of power struggle?
No one knows that yet.
Russia has been very suspicious of the official claim that a man who has been praised as a possible president suddenly wants to lead Russia's environmental policy.
The Kremlin's website presents Ivanov's words to Putin, in which he says: "It is true that at the beginning of 2012 I asked you to give me responsibility for this very complex and, to some extent, tricky task for four years.
Now I've been the president's head of administration for four years and eight months."
Ivanov took office in December 2011.
Until then, he served as Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Defence.
He is a member of the Russian Security Council and, like Putin, a former member of the KGB.
When Putin served as the head of the KGB Federal Security Service (FSB) in the late 1990s, Ivanov was appointed his deputy.
When Putin came to power, he named Ivanov as one of the five people he trusted the most.
It was once thought that Ivanov might come after Russian President Putin's second term, as the constitution would have prevented Putin's election for a third term.
However, the mission went to Dmitry Medvedev, someone close to Putin.
Putin became prime minister, after which he returned as president just three and a half years later.
Criminal Minds ‘The FBI Investigators’ star Thomas Gibson was fired after hitting the producer
Gibson was involved in a violent quarress in Los Angeles last month.
ABC Studios and CBS Television Studios announced the dismissal in a joint statement.
Gibson, known as Special Agent Aaron Hotchner, had issued an apology on Thursday before his dismissal was announced.
"We had creative disagreements.
I'm sorry that this happened," he said in a statement.
Details of how Gibson's character will be written out about the series following FBI agents will be released later, according to a statement from ABC Studios and CBS Television Studios.
Gibson, a 54-year-old who previously appeared in the comedy series Dharma & Greg, has been featured in the series since its first season, 2005.
"I love Criminal Minds and I've put my heart and soul on it for the last twelve years," Gibson said in a statement.
"I was hoping I'd see until the end of the series, but it's no longer possible.
I would like to thank all the writers, the producers, the actors, our amazing production team and the most important ones: the best fans the series could have ever wished for."
The 12th season of the series will start in the United States on September 28.
A mysterious landing of fish in the Banffin Gardens
Kevin Bain, who lives in Banff, has asked for help to identify the fishes that appeared in his garden.
Bain suspects these 75 fishes are swirls that have been stranded on the land by a whirlwind.
Bain, who lives 500 meters from the seashore, posted footage of the vibe in Periscope and hoped for a response to the appearance of these five-centimetre fish.
Queensferry's overpassage has now been annexed to Fife
The 1.35 billion-pound project is now scheduled to be completed by May 2017.
Engineers have annexed Queensferry's overpassage north deck and ground bridge, which means that the bridge is now connected to Fifee.
It is the first of the four sections between the "deck fans", which are now almost ready around the three pillars of the bridge.
The bridge section of Fife is now 600 metres long and weighs 30 000 tonnes.
It contains 10 000 tonnes of steel and 20 000 tonnes of concrete and 46 slanted ropes.
The 1.35 billion-pound project is now scheduled to be completed by May 2017.
Keith Brown, the cabinet minister responsible for the economy, visited the construction site today and was one of the first to walk from the ground to the bridge.
Brown says: "This is a historic and symbolic moment in the construction of Queensferry's overpass.
We are all witnessing a really significant step forward in the construction of the bridge. More than 30 000 concrete and steel have been used for the construction of this section of the bridge alone.
Despite its massive size and weight, the construction of the bridge section between the ground bridge and the bridge deck is a discreet operation that requires extremely precise installation tolerances.
Almost 79% of the entire bridge deck is now in place, and the rest of the lid is about to receive its concrete deck casting in Rosyth today, which means that the lid is ready to be lifted into place."
Michael Martin, head of the Forth Crossing Bridge Constructors project, says: "The completion of the first section of the bridge is always a significant urinary pole in all bridge projects.
In this fantastic project, this section represents the pinnacle of civil engineering.
When the last part of the road deck was installed and connected to the road cover of the North Tower, we then had to close the remaining north (i.e
This was done by dragging the northeast ground bridge 700 millimetres to the south.
This was a massive and at the same time high-precision operation.
Massive because the ground bridge is 222 metres in length and weighs about 6 000 tons and had to be pulled up at an angle of about 3%.
And high-precision, because the tolerances that the team had to follow were very small – just a few millimetres.
Fortunately, everything went well."
Moelfre Bay Rescue Department talks about three-man miraculous rescue
Anglesey's three men who were stranded from their fishing boat were miraculously rescued.
Moelfre RNLI's sea rescue station sounded an alert on Friday at 13:20 local time when its crew spotted an accident in Moelfre Bay Bay.
Three men desperately tried to swam to safety while their boat continued their reckless journey without a pilot.
"We saw the boat going right next to them and we imagined the worst," says Vince Jones, who drives Moelfre's lifeboat.
"By that time, when we got to the water, the strong winds had fortunately transported the boat away from them."
A local fishing boat rescued two of the men, while the third, aged 60, was found exhausted clumming to the buoy and without a life jacket.
They were all boarded to a coastal rescue boat and transported to a rescue station.
Vince Jones says: "It's a miracle that these three men who have fallen into the water are still alive and completely fine.
There was an emergency extinguishing rope in the boat's engine, but for some reason it had detached from the man who was driving the boat during the crash.
They were really lucky.”
The Pokémon Go yacht brings health and happiness
It's fascinating to see how the world is changing.
Over the past decade, many have complained about why teenagers and children no longer go out to play, but prefer to stay inside to stare at their screens.
Pokémon Go makes people get off the couch and get moving.
Originally, screen time was only spent watching TV, then video games and, most recently, smartphones.
So it's great to notice that technology and outdoor exercise don't exclude each other.
Pokémon Go is a game app whose characters come from a video game created in the 1990's (of which a cartoon series, playing cards and other merchandises were later made).
Like many popular cultural phenomena, this also stopped a little before the new game became available in early July in the United States, Australia and New Zealand.
The launch in other countries was delayed due to huge interest, which overloaded the servers.
In just a few days, the Pokémon Go game's number of users surpassed Tinder and was almost as large as on Twitter.
So how does the game work?
In short terms, players go out in search of Pokémon on their smartphones, which are tracked with GPS. When a Pokémon is found, it can be "photographed" (with the help of the phone's screenshot) or "abducted".
Fascination has caught up with the generation who played the video game when he was younger and lifted his buttocks off the couch.
In the United States, the game has significantly increased the number of steps that an "average person" takes in a day.
The Washington Post recently reported on the Cardiogram app on the Apple Watch, which observed within two days of the game's launch that the number of people moving 30 minutes a day suddenly rose from 45% to 53 percent. Cardiogram tracks 35,000 users.
The company doesn't know which user is playing Pokémon Go, it can only see the raw data on the amount of exercise.
Many public health campaigns encouraging people to get off the couch and exercise for at least 30 minutes a day have echoed deaf ears because the campaigns have been boring and their advertisers have looked unhappy.
However, the importance of exercise should not be underestimated.
In those who move regularly, the likelihood of developing cancer or depression is lower.
Exercise helps with weight management, lowers blood pressure, prevents diabetes and brings a large number of other benefits.
The beauty of Pokémon Go is that it gets people out to do something they enjoy.
In this way, it achieves three basic things that support health – exercise, fresh air and the sun and joy.
It is even possible to make new friends during the yacht.
As usual, this phenomenon also includes disapproving buzzkills.
And yes, someone can walk towards the door when they're playing.
So, what about it?
It's a breakthrough done.
However, even though Fitbit-like smartphone apps were able to monitor your activity in the past, they didn't give you a reason to get going, as Pokémon Go does.
Novelty charm disappears, and use eventually decreases.
However, thanks to Pokémon's success, there will be more games where apps and virtual reality are combined with activity in the real world, which will increase the physical activity of yacht-going people and improve their fitness and health.
