Simone Biles won gold in women's gymnastics multi-match
Forget the pressure.
Forget the hype.
Simone Biles is immune to all of them.
Dynamic jumps.
Effortless at the boom.
Jaw-locating on the floor.
Brilliant in everything.
And now finally the Olympic champion too.
The 19-year-old gymnast won the multi-match championship on Thursday and increased the gap between himself and the rest of the world in the Olympic spotlight.
His total score of 62.198 was clearly better than that of the "Final Five" teammate Aly Raisman and Russia's bronze medallist Aliya Mustafina.
Simone Biles of the United States will appear at the boom in the women's gymnastics multi-match final on Thursday at the 2016 Summer Olympics in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil.
Biles became the fifth American woman of all time in a row and won the multi-match title to boost her reputation as the best of her generation, or even the best of all time.
Biles burst into tears as his total points of the finals became visible and his long road to this moment ended.
Achievement takes the sport to a whole new dimension and elevates him to the same class as other sports superstars, such as Michael Phelps:
Biles has been completely superior for the past three years. He has won 15 world championship medals, of which 10 gold medals, and earned them with such ambitious and punctual programs that 1984 Olympic champion Mary Lou Retton called him "the greatest gymnast of all time."
In Brazil, there was still one last race, which took place above all between Biles and the burden created by himself and excessive expectations, not so much between Biles and the rest of the world.
Returning home to Spring in Texas with less than a handful of gold medals would be perceived as a disappointment.
Simone Biles of the United States (left) and Aly Raisman will hug gold and silver in the women's gymnastics multi-match final on Thursday at the Rio 2016 Summer Olympics in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. 11.8.2016
Biles won the first gold medal on Tuesday as a dot on top of the i for the going-away party of Martha Karoly, who has coordinated the national team and is now retiring.
Although Biles argued that he had never thought about anything in advance when he was at the top for a long time, that's not entirely true.
Part of his permanto program – which includes a volt series known as his trump card – has been matched with Latin music, which could well be heard in the streets outside the Rio Olympic Arena.
It's not a coincidence.
Boorman found the girl when she was a kindergarten in the gymnastics hall where Boorman was coached.
He hasn't lost a single multi-match race since the summer of 2013, and the winning streak is likely to continue as long as Biles wants it to continue.
Despite the fact that Mustafina got into a small lead with two threads, it was like a mirage.
What was left were the boom and the permanto, where Biles is the defensive world champion.
He got back to the lead after receiving 15,433 points from the boom and crooked the result with 15.933 points from the floor.
Raisman hugged him gently as they waited for the final dots—which was a mere formality—and tears began to appear around Biles’ red, white, and blue glittering eyeliner, when the dream he once wrote in his scrapbook came true.
Raisman's performance was a minor rematch to the race four years ago, when he first shared third place but then lost the bronze medal to Mustafina in the re-run.
This time, the 22-year-old team manager, whom Americans call the team's "grandmother", was clearly ahead of Mustafina.
He sent kisses of flights to the public after his permanto program and thus emerged from a losing position as a winner, which sometimes seemed insecure.
As far as Biles is concerned, this 19-year-old's tweet earlier in the week has a wholly new meaning:
According to police, three of them listened to music in the car as the victim of a serial killer
Two women and one 12-year-old girl were killed in Phoenix's deadliest massacre in ten years. The victims sat chatting in the car and listened to music when they were shooted, according to a police statement released on Thursday.
Authorities said Angela Rochelle Liner, Stefanie R. Ellis and Ellis's daughtress Maleah were shotted in a car parked across the street on 12 June.
The shooter stood on the passenger side of the car, from where he fired a handgun for eight shots and then escaped with another car.
The police suspect that the triplet was not caused by the robbery, as the attacker did not take the handbags or the 2,900 dollars found in the possession of another adult victim.
Sergeant Jonathan Howard, a spokeswoman for the Phoenix police, said police are now investigating whether the killing is related to four previous attacks.
A total of seven people have been killed and two wounded in nine attacks, which have taken place mainly in Latin-American-populated areas within four months.
"What events have in common is that they've occurred in residential areas in front of their homes," Howard told KPHO, a subsidiary of CBS, earlier this month.
The suspect is believed to have a number of vehicles, including a brown Nissan from the late 1990s, a black BMW from the late 1990s and a white Cadillac or Lincoln.
Investigators don't want to reveal what made them conclude that the attacks are interconnected and that they're the same.
Ellis, 33, was taken to a hospital because of her injuries, where she later died.
Liner, 31, and Ellis's daughtress Maleah died immediately at the scene.
An eyewitness told police that the trio had often sat in the car listening to music.
In addition, the police released a bulletin stating that the shooting of a vacant convertible was only half an hour before the triple shooting. The shooter is believed to be the same in both cases.
Investigators found shell casings and recovered shards of a bullet from the car's seat.
The mother of a 6-year-old boy with microcephaly: ‘Things are getting better’
Like most 6-year-olds, Edmund Picciuto likes singing, toys and, of course, his mother.
However, unlike most other peers, Edmund has microcephalia, i.e. a congenital small-headed disorder caused by the genetic disorder of his mother Elizabeth Picciuto, of which he was not awared when she was pregnant.
"When I brought Edmund home for the first time, my first thought was that I couldn't get through this.
I won't get through this.
I'm not committed to this.
Which isn't true at all – of course you're committed to it," he
Elizabeth and her husband, Vincent, were told that Edmund might never recognize them and might never survive at all.
Some of the doctors went so far that they suggested placing the child in institutional care.
"My first reaction was that I didn't think there was such a practice at all," says Picciuto.
Edmund's walking with a walker.
Edmund sat up and didn't start crawling until he was 3, and he still doesn't talk.
But he's moving slowly at his own pace.
He walks assisted and rides a bicycle.
He likes to play with his two brothers and loves to sing.
Elizabeth introduces Edmund's invented sign scheme, with which she means "sing to me."
"We're teaching him sign language," Picciuto told CBS News.
However, it not only means the usual sign language, it also includes signs invented by Edmund itself.
There may be a number of different diseases behind microcephaly: hereditary, as in the case of Edmund, or viral infections during pregnancy, such as measles or zika virus.
There is great variation in symptoms and prognosis.
It is still too early to assess how it will affect the life of those affected by the Zika virus.
Picciuto recalls an emotional storm caused by an uncertain future.
"I kept saying, what's going to happen,
“What will happen to us.”
And she still doesn't know.
"I haven't asked for a prognosis in a long time, because I don't think anyone can give it."
But Picciuto has hopeful advice for mothers whose children have congenital defects caused by the Zika virus.
"Things are getting better.
Things tend to work out.
“You will love your child and your child will love you.”
Heavy rains and flooding caused major rescue operations in the states of Louisiana and Mississippi
Louisiana's governor on Friday declared a state of emergency due to heavy rains and widespread flooding. More rain is expected in the state on Saturday.
According to national weather services, several rivers in Southeast Louisiana and Southern Mississippi were flooded and spread to a wide area as a result of heavy rain.
Louisiana's governor, John Bel Edwards, said state authorities are in constant contact with local authorities and help is already on its way to flood zones.
Mike Steele, a spokesman for the Government's National Security and Emergency Preparedness Administration, says the agency receives requests for flooded vehicles, boats and sand sacks.
According to Steele, Tangipaho County alone has asked for tens of thousands of sandbags.
The flood warning will remain in place until Saturday in the majority of southern Louisiana.
According to the weather service's press release, there may be 76–127 mm more water entering the area.
A flood warning was issued in southern Alabama on Friday as the rains continued in the Mobile region.
During the weekend, the Comite River near Baton Rouge and the Amite River near Denham Springs, both of which in Louisiana are predicted to rise to record heights.
According to weather forecaster Alek Krautmann, many houses along the rivers could be flooded in Baton Rouge suburbs.
In addition, he says that floods downstream in Ascension County pose a threat, as flooded rivers are slowly emptied into Lake Maurepas.
The Tickfaw river, flowing south of Mississippi's state border in Lousiana's Liverpool as soon as 9 a.m., had already risen higher than ever on Friday at 9 a.m.
The rescue work continued in Amite's and Wilkinson's counties in the south-western-Mississippi districts.
Leroy Hansford, his wives and stepson, were among those rescued near Gloster earlier on Friday.
Hansford, 62, reports that the Beaver Creek River, which is normally more than 120 metres from his house, rose rapidly during the night.
The other stepson, who lived nearby, had warned him.
"We got up, and the water came with a constant supply," Hansford says.
"It stretched to my waist."
The wife had told Hansford that she had never seen the river rise so high during the 48 years she has lived in the area.
Hansford says that he himself and his family members all have injuries, and he himself is the only one to swim.
Rescuers had rescued all three of the large military-style truck and taken them to a fire station in Gloster, where they kept shelter on Friday.
Weather forecaster Krautmann says the floods will spread rapidly as heavy rains continue, and authorities are already considering evacuation orders.
He talks about information received from an observer near Livingston, according to which almost 350 mm had fallen between midnight and Friday morning.
Krautmann says the soil got wet sharply after the rains that began on Wednesday.
Authorities report that due to heavy rains and street flooding, residents of Tangipaho County had to be rescued from their homes and training hours had to be cancelled in five school districts.
A member of Tangipahoa City Council had told WWL, a subsidiary of the television and radio company CBS, that there were 200 homes flooded.
Residents had been evacuated to two churches, but now the influence has reached both of them.
The county's leader, Robby Miller, said authorities had rescued 72 people and seven pets who had been captured by the flood.
Refuges have been opened to the cities of Amite and Hammond.
"They will be able to return to their homes when the water begins to retreat," he says.
Nearly twenty streets had to be closed due to a flood in the city of Hammond and residents were offered sandbags where they could try to prevent water from getting into their homes and business premises.
"The last time we experienced a major flood was in March," says Lacy Landrum, a member of the City Board.
"This influence seems similar."
In Mississippi, the rains continued steadily off the coast of the Gulf of Mexico, according to Rupert Lacy, rescue director of Harrison County.
Lacy said on Thursday a handfull house was reported, but none had been reported on Friday.
Keith Townson, the head of the Shopper Value Foods store, says he has lived in Amite for 40 years.
"I've seen water in places where I've never seen it before, and it's raining all the time."
Mahaj Brown, a 6-year-old who received several bullets in his bodies, is able to cope with the Philadelphia shooting
According to police, a 6-year-old son has been shot in Philadelphia, who is the second 6-year-old who has been shot in the city this week.
The child's grandparents told CBS Philadelphia the boy was Mahaj Brown.
According to the grandparents, the boy had several gunshot wounds in his groin, leg, arm and stomach, but he was conscious when he was taken to the hospital.
"He got many hits, his little body was full of bullets," the boy's grandmother Delores Melende said.
"I can't believe this."
The boy is in critical condition in the hospital and has undergone several surgeries.
The family tells the broadcaster that the boy is expected to survive.
He was about to start the first grade at a local elementary school.
Brown was one of the four people who were shot in the Germantown neighborhood.
Two 29-year-old men and one 32-year-old man were also the target of the shooting.
Another 29-year-old's condition was reported to be critical and the other two were stable, according to CBS Philadelphia.
According to the television company, the child was found in the front seat of the car next to the 29-year-old in critical condition.
The other two victims were not in the car, and police are now investigating whether one of the wounded men may have been the shooter.
Investigators told CBS Philadelphia it was a crucifixion between many armed men.
The venue was full of assault gun-type casings that originated from various assault weapons.
"It's quite clear that this was an ongoing battle.
There were casings on both sides of the sidewalk.
There have been a number of armed fighters, and they have been actively involved in the shooting," Philadelphia Police Chief Superintendent Anthony Ginaldi told a television station.
The bullets also hit many houses and vehicles in the area.
No arrests have been made and it is said that police are going through surveillance videos.
Another 6-year-old, who was the victim of a shooting in the city this week, was a girl whose hand was hitted by a stray bullet as she was playing outside her home on Tuesday in the West Oak Lane neighborhood.
The girl is recovering, and police are still looking for the suspect.
Why do we need Simone, Gabby and Laurie
This is what I call the women's gymnastics Olympic team, and there is more than one reason.
First of all, I've been at every Summer Olympic since 1984, and the United States has never been as overwhelming as it is now.
Secondly, the three gymnasts on this team, Simone Biles, Gabby Douglas and Lauren "Laurie" Hernandez, have inspired many colored girls.
The line-up of the team, complemented by Aly Raisman of the Jew and Madison Kocian of the Catholic, makes everyone proud.
The team finally represents America.
It has also confirmed the visibility of women of black and Latin background at the top of the sport.
For the past four years, the best female gymnast has been African-American.
Last year, Simone and Gabby were ranked 1 and 2.
Oh, it's amazing.
In the previous Olympics, Gabby Douglas emerged as the surprise winner of the Games, and the whole of America cheered.
Now the world has witnessed the incredible performances and surprising and masterious gymnastics programs of triple world champion Simone Biles, Douglas and Laurie Hernandez.
On Thursday, Simone became the fourth consecutive American to win gold in a multi-match of the women's individual competition.
Aly Raisman won the silver medal.
The views are at the top of the sport, but it doesn't look the same in gymnastic gymnastic classes nationwide.
Simone, Gabby, and Laurie followed in the same tracks as Aly and Madison to form an Olympic team.
They're the last five ("Final Five"), with only four gymnasts on the next gymnastics team, and this is Marta Karolyi's last year as coach.
However, the reality is that gymnastics is an expensive sport and gymnasts mainly train in private gymnastics clubs maintained by their parents. Parents have to pair training costs, facilities, trips, performance outfits, competition fees, hotel fees (for coaches, gymnasts and family members), etc.
The premises of most gymnastics clubs are located in prosperous residential areas that are able to support activities.
The teaching of gymnastics takes place in these clubs, and it means an expensive and long-term commitment to the families of gymnasts.
Simone Biles coach Aimee Boorman and Laurie Hernandez's coach Maggie Haney have both been coaching sports enthusiasts since they started gymnastics.
We need to arrange more affordable and better available gymnastics lessons for the countless young girls whose enthusiasm Simone, Gabby and Laurie have awakened.
I founded my own gymnastics foundation 20 years ago.
My organizations in Harlem have offered free or inexpensive gymnastics for more than 15 000 young people (mostly black and Latin-Americans), and some of these youths have achieved national and international championships.
In September, we will expand our operations in my hometown of Detroit so that we can offer high-quality gymnastics to young people who deserve the opportunity to learn and benefit from this great Olympic sport.
Gymnastic gymnastics develops strength, suppleness and coordination of the body and teaches hard work, discipline and determination.
This combination brings life-long benefits and supports good health and success in school and working life.
This team has aroused enthusiasm among the thousands of young girls who now want to start gymnastics, and it's absolutely amazing.
Participation in a sport is very important, especially for girls.
It's an exciting time.
Thank you Simone, Aly, Laurie, Gabby and Madison for the real "girlpower" you've shown when you won the Olympic gold medal, and for motivatininging so many young girls with different backgrounds to start their way into prosperous, strong, and successful young women.
The junior doctors rejected Jeremy Hunt's suggestion and are preparing for new strikes
Ministers hoped to end the controversy on the changes to the salary and working conditions by accepting a renewed agreement with the British Medical Association (BMA), which represents doctors, but 58 per cent of the so-called junior doctor who participated in the vote rejected the proposal.
In July, Health Minister Hunt announced plans to forcibly reach an agreement, saying the British public health (NHS) was "no one's country" and that any further delays would be harmful to staff and patients.
The first junior doctors will move to a new agreement that includes changes to the practices of night and weekend work in order to improve health care during antisocial times.
Ellen McCourt, chairman of BMA's Junior Medical Committee, issued a statement last night saying: "Junior doctors continue to be very cautious about the proposed agreement. A particular fear is that it will accelerate the current labor crisis and that it will not treat all doctors fairly.
It's only eight weeks until the first doctors are transferred to a new contract, so now we have to move on, because we're running out of time.
The BMA has tried to resolve the controversy by talking, but the government has been reluctant and sometimes unwilling to react in any way.
That's despite the fact that Jeremy Hunt made a promise last month that his door will always be open.
Jeremy Hunt needs to act now, overturn the implementation of the contract and respond to the concerns of junior physicians.
If this does not happen, junior doctors will be ready for further industrial action."
At the end of April, the previous strike postponed almost 13 000 routine procedures and 100 000 appointments when nurses and consulting physicians were asked to support striking physicians on strike.
Ministers hoped to end the controversy over the changes by concluding a renewed agreement with officials of a British trade union representing doctors.
The trade union BMA wants a better weekend salary for 54 000 junior doctors, who would be affected by the agreement.
Daniel Mortimer, CEO of the NHS employees, appealed to junior physicians not to embark on new strikes.
Mortimer says: "The industrial action achieves very little or nothing, but they put pressure on teams and services that are already at the limit and cause concern, distress and disruption to patients, medical staff and their families.
For the past two months, we have been conducting negotiations with the Junior Medical Committee and, together with the Ministry of Health and other bodies, we have welcomed their concerns about the role of the supervisor and the detection of grievances.
Employees were hopeful that a sustained positive commitment to other important issues – such as development, the flexibility of education, additional training for people returning from career breaks, training costs, mutual recognition of the educational programme, study leaves and the gender pay gap in the care sector – were a sign of how seriously the employees, Health Education England and the Ministry of Health want to stick with the BMA in November, February and May."
A medicine that costs about €2.40 per day can reduce the risk of a heart attack
Thousands of cardiac attack patients have access to a €2.40-per-day medication when the authorities recommended that it be given to more people and for long-term use.
Anti-coagulation drug tikagrelori reduces the risk of new heart attacks in patients with heart disease.
The drug is already given 12 months after a heart attack, which reduces the risk of a stroke or a new heart attack.
NICE (National Institute for Health and Clinical Excellence), a member of the UK's NHS and evaluating the introduction of new therapies, has instructed that tikagrelor should be administered to patients for a period of four years in order to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular problems.
But NICE (National Institute for Health and Clinical Excellence), a member of the UK's public health (NHS), has instructed patients should be given ticagrelor for four years in order to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular problems.
Approximately 140 000 people suffer a heart attack in England every year, a quarter of whom have a heart attack recurring or have a stroke.
Heart attacks and strokes are caused by fatty material that accumulates in the walls of the blood vessels and generates plaques.
If the plaque ruptures, it can give rise to a blood clot that prevents blood from entering the heart and causing a heart attack.
And if a blood clot comes off, it can travel with the bloodstream and block the flow of blood to the brain, resulting in a stroke.
The risk of a new attack is higher in people who have had a heart attack once.
Tikagrelor, produced by AstraZeneca in the UK and sold under the brand name Brilique, reduces this risk by reducing the likelihood of plugs.
The NICE's draft guidelines published today recommend that tikagrelor be administered for the next 90 mg for 12 months, after which the dose will be reduced to 60 milligrams, in addition, aspirin twice a daily for another three years.
Carole Longson, a professor at the Center for the Prevention of Treatment Methods (NICE), says: "Although effective treatment methods that effectively prevent the progression of the disease and the emergence of symptoms, up to a quarter of those with a heart attack have a new seizure or stroke – often with fatal consequences.
The fear of renewal can have a very negative effect on a person's quality of life.
We've been receiving evidence that ticagrelor combined with aspirin effectively reduces the risk of new heart attacks and strokes in people who have already had a heart attack.
A recommendation on the temporary use of tikagrelor will increase the treatment options for thousands of people who will benefit from it."
As data on the effectiveness and safety of ticagrelor, in particular as regards the risk of bleeding, are limited if used for more than three years, it is not recommended to use a treatment method for a longer period of time.
Due to the climate that fosters political correctness, voting fraud in Muslim areas flourished
Lutfur Rahman, the former mayor of Tower Hamlets, was expelled last year due to corruption, as a result of which the government filed a report
The climate that fostered political correctness led to the eye-closing of widespread electoral fraud in Muslim communities, according to a government report published today.
Former government minister Sir Eric Pickles severely criticized the police, election observers and city councils for turning a blind eye to election fraud because of "excellency towards ethnicity and religion."
According to Pickles, known as the former Minister of Regional State Administratives and current anti-corruption force, intimidating voters for religious reasons was so bad that the police should have been empowered to forming patrol chains outside the polling stations in order to protect intimidated voters.
He proposes that voters should present an identity card in order to prove their identity when they come to vote.
At the moment, voters only need to state their name and address.
In addition, the report proposes to dip their fingers in an indelible ink after voting so that no one is able to vote twice.
There should be more protection for reporters, says Sir Eric.
He demands that journalists' access to the Government's documents and the decisions of informal committees and working groups be improved.
"The aim is not only to prevent electoral fraud, but also to combat corruption and fraud against economic interests and the culture that favours them more widely in local governments," he says.
Britain's prime minister requested a report after a Voting Scandal for Tower Hamlet in East London.
Last year, a special electoral court determined that the then mayor Lutfur Rahman should be dismissed from his duties due to corruption and a number of illegal practices.
The court ruled that Rahman "scornfully misrepresented" the religious sentiment of his Muslim community and silenced his critics by accusing them of racism and anti-Islamism.
Rahman was said "that his campaign is based on the fact that it was the religious duty of every believer Muslim to vote for him."
As many as 300 of the votes were questionable or fraudulently voiced so that the person voted by pretending to be another person.
According to Sir Eric, evidence of fraud was obtained from all over the country.
He expressed his particular concern about postal election fraud, in which high-ranking members of the Muslim community were said to have pressured people to vote for a candidate of their choice.
Sir Eric (pictured) made a number of proposals to tidy up the voting system, including requiring an identity card at the polling station.
The report states: ‘Evidence has been provided that some vulnerable members of ethnic minority groups, in particular women and young people, have been pressured to vote in accordance with the will of older members, particularly in communities with a background in Pakistan and Bangladesh.”
"We were concerned that... state institutions had turned a blind eye to such activity because of the "politically correct" hypersensitivity to ethnicity and religion."
Sir Eric made 50 proposals to clean up the system, including a ban on the handling of mailballs by political activists in order to prevent "vote harvesting."
The report currently under review by Theresa May also calls for stricter checks on registration in order to prevent the use of the voting register for immigration and benefit fraud.
Consideration of alternatives in which voters would be required to present an identity card before voting.
Baptizing your fingers in inadmissible ink after voting, so that no one is able to vote twice.
Systematic checks to ensure the nationality of voters and their right to vote.
Setting a three-year limit for requests for automatic postal voting.
Prohibition of the handling of filled-in votes sent by mail and postal voucher envelopes from political activists.
For the police, the right to form guard chains around polling stations.
Changing the Act so that it is easier to file intimidation charges under the 1983 Representation of the People Act on the Expansion of Votical Rights.
Prohibition of photography at polling stations.
Only allowing English at polling stations (with the exception of Wales).
Enhancing the training to ensure that the staff supervises the rule, according to which only one person is allowed to enter the voting booth at a time.
Sir Eric harshly criticizes the London police and finds it "amazing" that no criminal charges were filed after the Tower Hamlet case.
"The Electoral Tribunal dismissed Lutfur Rahman and his spokesman for his role due to corruption and a number of illegal practices," he said.
"He was found guilty without any reasonable suspicion, and the threshold for criminal prosecution was exceeded on many grounds."
He also criticized the ineffective "check-up checks" of departments responsible for the City Council's electoral lists.
"The court's decision last year in the Tower Hamlet case was a wake-up call," said Sir Eric.
"Our country prides itself on its heritage as the mother of all parliaments, but this good reputation is threatened by the worrying and coveted electoral fraud of some parties and the denial of it."
