CBC's Olympic Commentator Apologizes for Saying Chinese Swimmer "Dead Like a Pig
Byron MacDonalds' analysis of 14-year-old Ai Yanhan's fourth place in the women's 4 x 200-meter freestyle relay dismastrated viewers of CBC's direct Olympic commentary on Wednesday.
The Canadian public broadcaster CBC has been forced to apologise since one of its commentators said the 14-year-old Chinese swimmer "lost the smells" and "dead like a pig" during the race.
Byron MacDonald thought his microphone was turned off when she began to shot her own opinions at the end of the women's 4 x 200-meter freestyle relay final.
MacDonaldin, who was included in CBC's commentary team to bring color, was heard saying: "This little 14-year-old Chinese egg, baby.
He was too excited, left fast and died like a pig.
"Thank you for that."
Chinese swimmer Ai Yanhan, 14, swam at 1:57.79 in the second leg of the relay and finished 1.61 seconds behind Taylor Ruck in Canada, allowing the Canadians to swim into China.
Viewers from all over Canada reacted quickly after being shocked by the tone and language of the commentator.
Emmett Macfarlane tweeted: "Did the CBC commentator just say that a 14-year-old Chinese swimmer died like a swine???
The idiot didn't realize he was on the broadcast yet."
Ai Yanhan, a Chinese swimmer who swamped in the women's 4 x 200-meter freestyle relay, was described in the following words: "This little 14-year-old Chinese egg, baby.
Twitter was quickly flooded with the furious Byron MacDonald critical comments, and many called for his dismissal.
The tweeters didn't hold back their words when they heard how reckless Byron Macdonald was.
Tweeter Sarah Paradis wrote: "Wow #ByronMacDonald.
You're representing the CBC and Canada, and you're making us all sound like a racist group.
Scott Russel, who hosted the CBC broadcast, quickly apologized to MacDonald: "We're apologizing for a comment about the swimming performance.
It was an unfortunate choice of words, and we regret that this happened."
CBC eventually had to apologize more than 90 times to people who had tweeted in response to the comment.
Commentary colleague Scott Russel had to apologize on MacDonald's behalf.
The CBC then copied and apologized to the comments of more than 90 other tweeters complaining about MacDonald's speeches.
The TV company also quickly issued a public apology.
"We are sincerely sorry, for these statements and that they made the broadcast.
We quickly apologized to our viewers on last night's broadcast, and we also issued an apology to our social media followers.
For the sake of clarity, Byron's comments were related to the swimmer's performance, not him personally.
Nevertheless, they were unsuitable and the choice of words was regrettable, and Byron is very sorry for his words."
On Thursday night, MacDonald expressed his apologies in the broadcast and tried to defend his speeches by saying he didn't mean it was personal.
"I'd like to apologize for the comment I made last night after the women's relay," MacDonald said on CBC's Olympic broadcast.
"I referred to the swimmer's performance, not the person.
Needless to say, I didn't mean anything derogatory with my words, and I'm very sorry."
In Primark, a woman who tried on underwear in the middle of the store shocked customers
Customers didn't know where to look when a woman undressed in the middle of Primark to try on underpants.
He further aggravated the situation by assessing what he looked like in underwear, took them off his leg and put them back on the shelf.
He continued the tour and eventually tried four pairs of underpants at a shop at the Bouverie Place shopping center in Folkestone.
Located in Folkestone's Bouverie Place shopping centre, the Primark chain store where a woman tried underpants in front of everyone and put them back on the shelf
Jenny Davidson, a mother of two children in the store at the same time, says that after trying on underwear, an unknown woman dressed and walked out of the store without buying anything.
He said, "People didn't believe their eyes.
The woman stood in the middle of the store, took her underpants off the shelf, took off her own jerseys and underpants, and put them on.
He didn't try to do it discreetly, but he did it without any shame.
Everyone stared when he took the underpants he had tried off and apparently didn't like them because he put them back on the shelf.
He did it three more times before finally deciding he didn't like them for anything, got dressed and walked outside.
He didn't seem to care about the looks – and he didn't think of the person who would eventually buy the underwear he'd tried on."
Davidson, 33-year-old who lives in Kent town, continues: "How disgusting to think that someone will take home the underpants that someone else has worn and haven't been washed."
The incident took place Tuesday afternoon.
"I have understood that the store's staff tried to remove those garments from sale when the woman had left – what's right.
It would be terrible to take your new underwear home and notice that someone else has tried them without them being washed," adds Davidson.
Primark's spokesman commented: "Primark will be aware of the situation that occurred on Tuesday, 9 August.
All such products have been withdrawn from sale."
Here's how you're defending yourself against an armed attack with Krav Maga
The world is full of dangers, but this video from Dailymail.com can help make it a little safer.
We interviewed Rhon Mizrach, Grand Master of the Krav Maga in New York, and asked for advice on how to cope with a robber or abuser waving the gun in close proximity.
The video shows you how to defend yourself against the attacker according to whether the attacker is approaching you from the front or back.
If the attacker is approaching from behind, the first thing you have to turn around so that you can see the attacker and get away from the shooting line, says Mizrachi.
After this, grab the gun so that it gets caught between your forearm and upper arm, and strike the attacker in the face with your free elbow.
Then kick the attacker in the groin with his knee, and when he goes out of the pain double, slap him in the face with the barrel of the gun, and retreat by keeping the gun directed at him.
The technique is similar if the armed attacker is approaching from the front. You need to move away from the shooting line, grab the gun and twist it so that the attacker can't launch.
At the same time, place your weight on the gun and punch the attacker in the chest. Turn the gun away from the attacker's grip, retreat and keep the weapon aligned with the attacker.
Does that sound tricky?
Watch the video to see how Mizrachi does it.
However, keep in mind that the safest way to get rid of the robber by handing over your cash – unless you’re the Grand Master of Krav Maga.
Ian Wright, a world traveler making a travel guide, licks the dirtiest places in Europe
The British globetrotter put his resilience and gagging reflex to the test by licking Europe's dirtiest and most disgusting places.
Ian Wright, 51, a Suffolki-based presenter and travel guide writer, wanted to prove the strength of his resistance by licking the euro bracket, the handrail in Piccadilly Circus, the toilet rinsing button on the train and the Russian public phone.
Before licking, Wright measured the objects with a luminometer to see for themselves and to show the viewers where he was pushing his tongue.
The experiment was turned into a documentary film Ian Wright – On the Trail of Inner Power, and its results were surprising.
The idea of licking the flushing button on the train toilet can be disgusting, but in fact, more bacteria are in the kitchen sink.
On his journey in different corners of Europe, Wright interviews doctors and other medical professionals about strengthening good intestinal bacteria and their own health.
Fortunately, nothing he licked caused but a bad-tasting mouth.
Ian Wright originally became known for her travel program, where she tested her intestinal stamina by traveling around the world and eating strange and wonderful things.
Licking railway station handrails and toilet flushing button undoubtedly challenged Wright's resistance.
Wright says in the documentary: ‘I’ve been quite carefree since I was a kid, and I’ve put all sorts of things in my mouth, such as dirt, worms and mud water.’
In the movie, he really proved to be worthy of his reputation.
The border was only encountered in a filthy public toilet in Russia.
The luminometer measured more than 4000 units of the sample.
In comparison, the number of newly washed hands is usually less than 60.
The boundary came across in the Russian public toilet, where Wright refused to lick when the luminometer measured 4000 units – in newly washed hands, the same number is less than 60.
Wright explores Ian Wright, produced together with Valio, on the trail of Ian Wright, where the amount of bacteria is made visible in different places, such as the bathroom.
Wright says in the documentary: "This is almost the worst smell I've ever smelled in any toilet.
4000 units! Nothing is dirtier than that!
It's such a disgusting place that I really don't lick anything here."
Wright produced the documentary film Ian Wright in collaboration with Valio.
From Valio, Wright received Valio Gefilus containing lactic acid bacteria and vitamins C and D, which helps to strengthen the good bacterial strain and resistance of the intestines.
Niko Vuorenmaa, Valio's category manager for snacks, juice and novelty products, says: "We wanted to offer consumers something completely new.
Something that's fascinating to look at and at the same time tells about the benefits of probiotics.
We spent a lot of time brainstorming something crazy, and in the end we ended up the idea of asking Ia to come along."
Intestinal health has become a global trend.
"Instead of just talking about products, we wanted to bring them into a wider context and tell our daily environment about good and bad bacteria, the importance of gut health and how it relates to immunity.
Intestinal health has become a global trend, and with the help of the document, we can provide consumers with more information about it in an interesting way.
The film also speaks to our sense of humour!"
Tom Hakala, who directed the film, adds: "Let's just say I'd have lost respect for Ian if he'd licked certain items we'd asked for, such as the disgusting russian toilet, which is also shown in the movie.
Naturally it was easier for Ian to lick some objects, while others were pretty bad.
“I’m glad we were able to photograph some of the dirty licks on tape.”
In Valio's survey conducted in 2015, 84 per cent of the respondents linked lactic acid bacteria especially to the well-being of the stomach and intestines, and 58 per cent said they had used products with the addition of lactic acid bacteria at least from time to time.
Before eviction, the troublemaker family destroyed a city-owned rental house in Saltash, Cornwall
The troublemaker family filed 50 000 pounds of damage with the city-owned rental house after being evicted due to the terrorization of the neighborhood.
Tanya Skeldon and her husband Shaun Trebilcock have been evicted from their apartment in Saltash, Cornwall, following numerous complaints against the couple.
When the couple had been forcibly removed from the apartment, representatives of the rental housing company said that the apartment was in good condition for renovation.
There were gaping holes in the walls, the floorboards had been torn off, debris had been applied to the floors and the walls had been smeared with graffiti.
The couple have now moved and are believed to live in a private residence.
Donna McEvoy, head of the Cornwall Housing Agency (Cornwall Housing), says she fears the apartment may no longer be saved.
He says, "This has been a complete nightmare for the people who live here, no one should have to put up with this.
There's a lot to weigh in, as the property is likely to be known as a not-so pleasant part of Cornwall.
The family is infamous, just like its name."
The couple had three sons who lived in the apartment from time to time. According to the police, the family's territory included street fights with baseball bats, intimidation of nearby residents, drug use and caring, as well as loud shouting, arguing and swearing.
Skeldon, a 47-year-old who signed the lease, received an anti-social behavior order in June.
Old waste was found in several rooms of the house under threat of demolition.
Drugs had been sold from the house in the past, and the floorboards had been torn off.
According to the neighbors, the family fought on the street and the police had to be called regularly.
Cornwall's Housing Agency evicted the couple by coercion this week after giving them 11 weeks more time to move out voluntarily.
Relieved neighbors were grateful for the eviction – they said that their lives had been hell on earth.
One of the neighbors said, "They didn't care about anyone.
We've been living next door to them for 20 years, and it's been hard all that time.
“The police have come here many times.”
"This morning we couldn't sleep.
We woke up because it was so quiet and we're not used to it!
It's going to be wonderful now that they've finally been evicted."
Adam Fitzpatrick, head of the Cornwall Housing Agency's forced operations, said the family was unable to work with local authorities.
The family lived in the house for 20 years, during which time the police were regularly called.
Rubbish and old toys had been throwned on the wild wall of the rental house.
Fitzpatrick adds: "It's a shame the tenant didn't take any responsibility for his own and his family's behavior, as eviction is always the last action the Cornwall Housing Department is considering.
This case was very difficult, as the behavior was extreme and their actions must have been shocking to look at the neighborhood.
I am grateful to my working group, the Devon and Cornwall police and community members, who participated in the work and boldly offered to provide us with information.
I hope that this progress will improve the well-being of our neighbours and give the message that anti-social behaviour will not be tolerated."
Fitzpatrick says that the family's three sons had all lived in the apartment at different times, but were already in their twenties.
Dirty mattress found in one bedroom of the house after eviction
Housing workers now need to clean out the heaps of waste that surround the house.
The police raided the apartment in 2014 and found more than 70 grams of cannabis, as well as scales and other supplies, as well as 1,700 pounds in cash.
Skeldon was fined after being found guilty of possessing a class B drug with the intention of selling it.
He claimed that the money was for the tombstone of Lee Skeldon, his son, who died in a car accident in 2013.
After becoming homeless, Skeldon and her husband have been heard to have moved to a private residence in Plymouth.
The walls of many of the rooms were smeared with graffiti.
Police say they're demanding eviction for tenants who violate the rules of leases.
Angela Crow of Devon and the Cornwall police say this eviction serves as a clear warning to tenants who are constantly defying the rules.
"This is a clear message for those who think they can behave however they want without prosecution, as well as for those who have to suffer such behavior," says Crow.
Devon and the Cornwall police will never accept criminal and anti-social behaviour, and will continue to work with their partners to protect our communities.
An army jet frightening tourists in Portugal
Sun-worshippers experienced the shock of their lives in Aveiro, Portugal.
The Portuguese Air Force's P-3C Orion aircraft plunged staggeringly low.
According to the Air Force, it was a training flight that did not threaten safety.
The picture shows the moment when tourists went into panic when a low-flying jet plunged over a full beach.
Dramatic images show an army airplane coming almost in contact with tourists sunbathing on a sandy beach in Aveiro in northern Portugal.
The vacationers got on their feet, understandably worried about the thunder of the jet's engines, which began to be heard before they saw what was coming through the clouds.
The incident took place yesterday afternoon in Costa Nova near Aveiro.
One of the vacationers who filmed the dramatic fly-by and posted the videos on social media was Daniel Fernandes, who said: "It overtook the beach twice really low, and it was the moment when it looked like it was rushing to the ground."
Nuno Arroja adds: "I was surfing and I was sure the plane would fall on me."
Tourists on Aveiro's beach were shocked when the plane suddenly appeared and flew surprisingly low.
The Portuguese Air Force announced today that the pilot of the P-3C Orion aircraft was on a training flight where the purpose was to identify coastal fishing vessels, which is why the aircraft had to flew so low.
According to the pilot, they had to fly a little lower than normal, because the smoke caused by nearby forest fires hampered visibility, but according to the Air Forces, this did not threaten anyone's safety.
Forest fires have wreaked havoc in parts of mainland Portugal and killed four people on the island of Madeira.
The Portuguese Air Force announced today that due to heavy fog and poor smoky visibility, the pilot had to fly so low.
The plane was on a training flight, during which the pilot was asked to identify the fishing vessels.
Eksote's first-half result better than expected
The South Karelia Social and Health Care District (Eksote) made a better result of 3.4 million euros in January-June.
Eksote's result showed a surplus of 4.3 million euros in the first half of the year.
The result was particularly good in family and social services, where the development of preventive services has been able to dismantle institutional care.
The care guarantee was achieved in all activities.
Eksote aims to shorten the sequence of measures this year.
Eksote covers the deficit previously created by the surplus.
The leftover portion may be used to shorten queues and purchase procedures for patients.
The full-year result is projected to exceed 4.6 million euros than planned.
Eksote's Board of Directors will discuss the six-month report at its meeting next Wednesday.
70 Special Border Jaegers Sworn Military Oath in Immola
70 conscripts of the Border and Coast Guard School vowed a military oath on Friday.
A military oassing ceremony for the conscripts of the Border and Coast Guard School was held in Immola on Friday.
The 10th Special Border Jaeger course took place at the garrison's sports field.
The military oath swored 70 conscripts.
Special Border Jaegers are trained for demanding border security, reconnaissance and combat missions.
They're serving for 347 days.
This year, 310 young people applied to become Special Border Jaegers.
250 of them were invited to the entrance examinations.
Special Border Jaegers have been trained in Immola since 2007.
The veteran's meeting during the ceremony was attended by five veterans of the war-time border forces.
The average age of those involved is now 90 years.
The oldest veterans who participated in the event were born in 1918.
A bit ahead of the bandit - criminal intelligence helps to solve crimes and exposes serious crime - ESS.fi
From the beginning of 2014, each of Finland's 11 police departments has had an RTA, i.e. forensic intelligence and analysis unit.
The unit's action is invisible on the outside.
The Häme Police Department consists of a total of four police officers in Lahti and Hämeenlinna.
It's headed by a Sergeant located in Lahti
The superintendent is the Chief Inspector in Lahti.
In addition, the unit's strength includes a civilian dedicated to information technology in Hämeenlinna.
Everyone's working in a civilian uniform.
The unit has proven its usefulness and necessity, says Deputy Police Chief Tero Seppänen.
The RTA unit produces various clarifications for the rest of the police.
In Häme, among other things, it has acquired information on asylum seekers.
The most recent reform of working methods
The establishment of analysis units is the most recent reform of the police's working methods.
The aim has been to prevent crimes, i.e. the fact that the police would rarely go to the swirls only when they receive an alarm.
With the information collected and analysed by the unit, day-to-day police activities are targeted where it is most needed.
According to the reform of the police administration, one criminal intelligence centre was established in the country, less than three national units, and its own criminal intelligence unit for each police department.
The PTR Centre, which operates in connection with the National Bureau of Investigation in Vantaa, manages operations nationwide.
As the name suggests, PTR units employ people from the Police, Customs and the Finnish Border Guard.
RTA units, on the other hand, work in areas divided according to police departments, and their task is to provide information to both local police forces and national units.
Inquiry extends beyond its own territory
While an individual police officer is aware of what has happened during his 12-hour shift, it is the RTA unit's job to know what's going on in the whole area of the police department.
The inquiry also extends beyond the boundaries of its own institution, because so does crime.
Employees working in the RTA unit have better computer skills than the basic police.
With the help of registers, an alarm task database and observations, the unit aims to draw the attention of the police to new phenomena, prollent criminals or crimes that resemble each other.
This is called serialization, and it's one of the most important tools of criminal intelligence.
All people have their own way of working.
The handprint is also visible at work, whether it's honest or criminal.
Based on the method, a certain image of the perpetrator is formed.
Serialization can be carried out either on the basis of the method or on the basis of the person, depending on which the police have more information about.
Criminals learn from their mistakes
The employees of the RTA unit do not walk around the city with their collars up, but their work is not entirely public either.
The methods used both by themselves and criminals are not disclosed, as it could lead to professional criminals developing their activities in order to prevent attempted crime prevention by the police.
Of the methods, the police will say publicly if it anticipates that they will pose a threat.
For example, in early summer, such a case had been reported, the means of marking vacant dwellings used in apartment burglaries.
In it, the perpetrators put a stick, a pin or a piece of tape on the front door of the apartment, with which they tried whether the residents were really traveling.
The pre-trial material shall be made public at least to the parties concerned.
The analysis unit has found that the most professional criminals learn from the mistakes they've made.
The most clever people order the district court's minutes, which may indicate, for example, that fingerprints have been found on the site.
The next time we'll go to the thieves with our gloves on.
For the purpose of the study, Deputy Police Chief Tero Seppänen from the Häme Police, Inspector Tuomo Lepola from the Eastern Finland Police, Detective Inspector Sami Joutjärvi from the Joensuu Police Station and Detective Inspector Arto Tuomela from the National Bureau of Investigation have been interviewed.
The police collect and analyse information that is used to plan practical activities.
A reduction in the amount of labour and funding.
The first steps towards a new course of action were taken 30 years ago.
The latest change was the three-stage PORA reform, which ended at the end of 2013.
The national cooperation collects information from outside the borders of its own institution and Finland.
AN ISIS schoolgirl was killed in Syria
According to the news, one in three British schoolchildren who joined ISIS in the last year, Kadiza Sultana, has been killed in Syria.
Attorney Tasnime Akunjee said the girl's family heard that Sultana died in Raqqa, Syria, a few weeks ago.
He was believed to have been killed in a Russian airstrike in Raqqa, an ISIS base in Syria, ITV News reported on Thursday.
