Vinyl charm: who buys records?
Lonely, middle-aged men like vinyl.
Before you have time to write an angry objection in the comment section under the pseudonym EiväAllLongishMiddle-age Men, this claim is based on actual research data.
According to the YouGov research company, behind the much-discussed re-entry of vinyl records is not a millenial-generation boom, when millennials want to feel a physical object in their hands, but instead there is midlife nostalgia in the background.
The age of the person who recently bought the vinyl album is most likely between 45 and 54.
Those belonging to the age group 18-24 are, in fact, the least likely buyers.
It’s also not just a question of enthusiasts’ desire to collect records, it’s an emotional meaning: older vinyl record buyers are a little more likely to keep their emotions hidden (56% in 53%) and enjoy being alone (69% in 66%).
But are YouGov's results true?
If this blog were a televised news broadcast, the camera would accompany me as I walked along the lively Soho Street, wearing an unpretentious grey jacket. I would gesture lively, bend my hands into a cup and say something traditional, such as, "Let's find out."
So let's find out.
Although Soho has slowly lost its charm and ruggedness, as hotels, Eats, Prets and Paperchase chains have taken over the premises owned by private entrepreneurs, there are still a handful of special record stores left.
I'd probably ask the photographer to stop the camera for a moment as I get very close to the men visiting these shops and by observing to assess their age and character.
Stuart from Glasgow, 55, followed by Sister Ray (at the momen, two young women, three middle-aged men) to Reckless Records (with eight middle-aged men, probably a woman in her twenties) is on a conference trip in London during the afternoon and spends her extra time getting to know what the shops have to offer.
I'll ask if he's a collector.
‘I think I am,’ he said.
"I've got about 3000-4000 records."
These plates fill his living room walls, as you can see from the picture. (Stuart sent a picture to me by e-mail, I didn't follow him home.)
The reason why he has acquired such a large number of albums and singles is partly on his disposable income. He is now able to buy records that he could not afford when they originally appeared, and to buy again the things he had had to sell when he was young and moneyless.
"I got a lot of stuff from the late 60's and early 70's that came out when I was 11 or 12 years old and probably too young to get them," he says.
He agrees that those who buy vinyl records are more likely to be introverts who enjoy their own company, and add: "I don't smoke, and I don't drink much.
It's my vice."
Wez, 25, who works at Sister Ray, also believes that many of their customers will fit the YouGov profile.
However, he has noticed a new kind of flow of people that has been influenced by media hype. These people have heard about the arrival of vinyl records again and now feel compelled to buy their old albums back.
"Based on the conversations I've had, people had generally given up their collections around 1998 or 1999.
Customers who sold their vinyl to buy CDs are now selling their CDs to buy back their vinyl," he says.
This may explain the vinyl-waking boom of a certain age.
What about the alleged emotional significance of the records?
In just a few seconds, you'll realize in a movement like Phonica that they'll provide a comforting community for like-minded people.
According to Wez, some of the older customers are particularly upset about the contactless payment system.
The digital world may overwhelm some vinyl fans, for whom returning to familiar record cases means permanence and a reminder of the lucky days of youth.
"I think it sometimes fills a gap or material property is acquired as some kind of consolation," says Wez.
"This is how I experience it myself as a collector, and I believe that so do many others.
“It’s an easy way to get your mind elsewhere.”
"We're like social workers!" says Wez's co-worker from behind the record stack.
An ICM survey conducted in April revealed that almost 50% of those who bought a vinyl album in the previous month had not yet listened to their album.
An opinion poll also reported that 41% have a turntable that they never use, and 7% of vinyl record buyers don't have their own turntable at all.
At this point in the news broadcast, I'd probably show a serious face while browsing the stacked bossa nova collections.
There is also a "trophy phenomenon" related to the new arrival of vinyl records, discovered by Jonny, 42, a saleswoman of the Sounds of the Universe (at the moment three middle-aged men).
"A customer recently came to the store and said he didn't have a turntable, but he wants to buy a Radiohead disc so he can put it on his shelf," Jonny said.
"The share of such customers is not high, but they still exist.
For them, materiality is more important than music.
Some of our clients say they don't have a record player yet.
They're younger, not older, mostly teenagers who're just starting a hobby."
The customer base is much more varied in Fopp, where I will arrive next. The store has dedicated an entire layer to vinyl records.
Customers include couples in their 70s, tourists who take photos, teenage boys and middle-aged women.
The diversity of the customers is delightful, even though the atmosphere is not as relaxed as in the previous stores: instead of swinging jazz, the loudspeakers are roared by Wild Beasts' latest synthesizer pop.
None of the clients look particularly lonely, but it's hard to detect gnawing psychological trauma by standing next to someone for 30 seconds.
Elanora, 27, has been walking around Fopp for a few minutes and was looking at various discs on sale.
He's looking rather than shopping.
He says his income is low, so it's not possible for him to collect records.
"They cost a lot.
It's easy to listen to music from a computer or otherwise, but the beauty of wine is . . . .", he lets out a delighted sigh and continues:
"I can't explain, they're so unique."
My last stop is a store that is said to have inspired a new generation of vinyl fans: Urban Outfitters.
I notice a bunch of teenage girls you'd think you're going on denim shorts, but they'll gather for a while around Polaroid cameras, and I'm going to see a father and daughter thinking about buying a Crosley record player.
On the left is a staircade whose wall is filled with Adele, Jeff Buckley, Amy Winehouse and Fleetwood Mac: a mixture of contemporary music and classics, key albums for any average collection.
No one looks at the wall, perhaps because they are just an element of decoration and a part so high that they don't even reach.
They're pure "art vinyl" decor.
However, considering that all the means to get attention to these plates may mean more money for the industry and send a few customers on a search trip to Soho, there's not much to complain about.
It's no surprise that the population group that's most likely to have more time and money than most is also the group that spends the most money on luxuries, such as vinyl records.
Although my research shows that YouGov's results are true, at least in Soho's record stores, they don't exclude a large number of female collectors and vinyl fans, which actually exist both in record stores and online forums.
These people are likely to be closely attached to school or working life.
I'm sure they won't spend their Thursday mornings wandering Berwick Street's record stores, browsing records, and avoiding the 30-year-old who's pretending to be a television presenter, staring frighteningly and looking at unsuspensible middle-aged men.
Holkeri, i.e. ice shark, is the longest-living vertebrate – video report
According to scientists, the longest-living vertebrate on Earth is the ice shark.
