A French court rejected an application to dismantle the jungle camp's shops
A French court has dismissed an application from the Calais authorities calling for dozens of temporary shops and restaurants to be dismantled in a jungle camp inhabited by thousands of refugees.
During this week's trial, the Calais authorities filed allegations that the state should be allowed to break up 72 temporary shops set up in the refugee camp. Stores sell the residents of the refugee camp a variety of items from cigarettes to energy drinks and offer haircuts. Tea can be bought from temporary restaurants for 50 cents or basic meals for a few euros.
According to Calais prefect authorities, these shops and restaurants must be dismantled because they do not pay taxes and therefore represent the shadow economy and are associated with fire and hygiene risks.
Humanitarian auxiliary groups told the court that shops and restaurants are essential for the camp, as free meals provided by a state-subsidized organization and other parties are not enough for an ever-growing group of people in the camp.
According to them, temporary shops and restaurants often provide shelter and free food for those who need them.
Lille's court ruled that there was no legal basis for the expulsion of 72 temporary traders, but considered Calais' prefect's concerns to be "comprehensively understandable".
According to Judge Jean-François Molla, the camp's grocery shops, cafés and restaurants are of much greater importance than just serving food to people who "live in extremely unstable conditions."
He says they offer peaceful places where refugees and volunteers can meet each other.
One of threatened places under threat of dismantling was the camp's Jungle Book Kids café, which is non-proficient and offers 200 meals a day, classes in English and French, and asylum advice for hundreds of unaccompanied minors whose position is the most vulnerable.
More than 170 000 people have subscribed to a petition calling for a children's café.
According to a letter sent to the Guardian, its closure "would be a disaster for these children, some of whom are only 8-year-olds."
Two charities, the Help Refugees and the L'Auberge des Migrants, estimated on Friday that after a sharp increase in the number of visitors this summer, the number of people in the jungle camp has risen to a record number of more than 9 000 people.
The price of food in Finland will fall clearly faster than the EU average
In Finland, food is sharply reduced to the delight of consumers and annoyance of producers.
The price of food in Finland has fallen by almost five per cent in three years, while on average in EU countries its price fell by 0.9 per cent.
At the same time in Germany and Sweden, food has become one per cent more expensive.
Over the past three years, the price of food in Finland has fallen to the second highest level among EU countries with the same level of wages as Finland.
Of the comparison countries only in the UK, the price of food has fallen faster than in Finland.
The data can be found in Statistics Finland's Development Manager Ilkka Lehtinen's article, which was published in the Tieto & Trends blog.
Lehtinen compared real prices, i.e. prices that are proportional to the general development of consumer prices.
The latest data are from June this year.
At the beginning of the 2010's, the price of food rose sharply.
In 2013, prices climbed close to the 1994 price level.
The situation was alarming.
"We were close to losing the price advantage that came with EU membership," Lehtinen says.
Then followed by a turnament, after which the food has become cheaper every year.
The price is now weighed down by Russia's bans on imports of food, which have stopped food exports from Finland to Russia.
Therefore, the supply of milk and meat products on the Finnish market has increased and producer prices for agriculture have declined.
"Putin's policy has helped the Finnish consumer in the short term," Lehtinen says.
Intensified price competition between industry and commerce has also contributed to the fall in prices.
The large retail chains S Group and Kesko have both lowered their prices.
For the consumer, price competition has been reflected in spectacular advertising campaigns.
However, Finland is still not a haven of inexpensive food, even though the price of food has fallen extensively.
In 2015, food in Finland was the fourth most expensive in the EU countries, which had the same level of wages as Finland.
According to Lehtinen, the price of food sold in Finland is significantly influenced by VAT.
He found that if the reference countries had the same VAT rate, third-lowest food would be sold in Finland from countries with the same level of wages.
Lehtinen compared Finland to Denmark, Sweden, Austria, France, Italy, Britain, Germany and the Netherlands.
If the VAT rate were seven in the peer countries, only Germans and the Dutch would sell cheaper food than Finns.
"If we want to get to the level of the old EU countries in the price of food, we need to halve VAT to the average level of EU countries and continue price competition," Lehtinen writes.
Lehtinen points out that the calculations he has made at the VAT rate are indicative.
The school isn't quite the same anymore
Returning to the school bench may notice that there has been a change in the school during the summer.
A new curriculum has been drawn up for schools at the beginning of the semester in all primary schools.
It is a guideline according to which all Finnish schools operate.
Schools decide the details themselves, but the plan indicates the official goals.
New instructions have been prepared for several years, and they differ from the old instructions in many ways.
Among other things, learning fiction is abandoned, and instead schools start practicing coding.
Swedish education begins in the sixth grade at the latest, and social studies begin to be studied already at four.
However, the biggest change concerns the role of students.
It used to be thought that the teacher teaches the students, but now everyone should be active and learn from each other.
In this way, we try to make schooling more interesting.
The world's longest vertebrate was found - an ice shark can live up to 400 years
Known as ice sharks (Somniosus microcephalus) do not reach sexual maturity until the age of 150.
It can live for another 250 years after that, a new study reports.
The figures make the species the longest-lived vertebrate in the modern world.
In the animal kingdom, it is overtaken only by an Icelandic mussel, the oldest known specimen of which is timed until the age of 507.
Greenland's sharks are swimming along the North Atlantic.
They also hold another record: they are among the largest fish in the Arctic.
Females stretch longer than males, they can grow up to more than five meters.
Since the pace of growth is slow, according to follow-ups, only about a centimeter a year, adult individuals have forcibly lived for a long time.
But for how long?
Julius Nielsen from the University of Copenhagen and his colleagues found out.
He determined the ages of adult ice sharks from 28 females who had been caught by fishermen.
The age of the fish was revealed in the radio charcoal timing of eye lenses.
The average age of the sharks studied turned out to be at least 272 years.
The sample's two largest fishes, 493 and 502-centimetre amazones, were uncovered at the age of about 335 and 392.
Since previous studies have found that ice branches only reach gentral maturity of more than four meters, the researchers calculated that they were at that stage around the age of 150.
The study was published by Science.
Stockmann's dismissal to nearly 300 - sellers will not be deducted
Nearly 300 employees will be laid off from the department store company Stockmann.
The co-operation companies ended at the beginning of August.
