He performed as an athlete, gave a urine sample, and even signed the documents.
"We can't accept that kind of behavior," Kip Keino, chairman of the Kenyan Olympic Committee, thundered to Reuters.
Athletics at the Olympics will start on Friday and Rotichi is scheduled to run at that time.
It is not yet known whether the runner is aware of the consequences of the incident.
Rotich finished fourth at the World Cup last year on the way and is one of Rio's favourites.
According to Reuters, a source inside the Kenyan athletics team claimed that Anzrah went with an athlete's accreditation card only to get free meals in the competition village.
When he was encountered by anti-doping officers, they assumed he was an athlete and he was taken to the test.
The coach did not explain the situation to the officers because he was afraid of being exposed," the source said.
Where's everybody?
Rio's athletics stadium is empty
Where's everybody, anyway?
Certainly not at the Rio Olympic Stadium following athletics.
In Rio on Friday, a struggle for athletics medals began, but the world's top individuals were allowed to showcase their skills to a confusingly empty grandstand.
Rio organizers said on Friday that only 58 per cent of first-day morning tickets and 65 per cent of evening tickets have been sold.
Four years ago, there was another in London, where the station was bulging with cheering crowds.
Tickets for the finals have already been sold out in Rio.
Spectator loss at the Rio Olympics has attracted attention even before athletics.
Thousands of empty benches have been seen in the grandstands of rugby, paddling and riding, for example.
While Brazil's medal hopes Talita Antunes and Larissa Franca played beach volleyball in the legendary Copacabana, only 70 per cent of the stands were crowded.
After the Brazilian duo, the Netherlands played against Russia, and about 30 per cent of the 12 000 grandstands were only around 30 per cent.
Poor sales are at least explained by the fact that Brazil, which is struggling in financial difficulties, is far away for European and Asian race tourists, and many of the Olympic sports are bizarre for Brazilians who are used to football.
However, even the first match of the Brazilian football team could not fill the football stadium.
Mario Andrada, a spokesman for race organizers, said 82 per cent of tickets had been sold at the time, which is clearly lower than four years ago.
"There are still 1.1 million to be sold," Andrada said, according to Reuters.
Of the 8.5 million tickets at the London Olympics, 8.2 million were sold, despite nearly half the cost of the tickets in Rio.
In Rio, ticket prices have ranged from about nine euros to more than a thousand euros in the opening ceremony.
More than half of the tickets cost less than 16 euros.
At the London Olympics, volunteers and military personnel were allowed to sit in empty seats, but in Rio this is not yet allowed because the organizers need money and are trying to sell tickets until the last moment.
In Rio, however, organizers have issued 240 000 tickets for schoolchildren in the country for lesser-known sports, such as golf and rugby.
There weren't many empty spots at Rio Stadium this morning.
Even beach volleyball on Copacaba's sand hasn't attracted any interest to the audience.
Jukka Keskisalo competed in London in 2012.
There weren't many people in the galleries.
A new way to protect nature - acquire the right to land
The Helsinki Foundation participates in nature conservation through new means.
Founded last year, the foundation acquires ecologically significant natural areas.
Subsequently, individuals, corporations and corporations can use money to claim holder rights to the land.
The foundation has acquired its first land area in Lapland.
The land area is virtually divided into blocks, which those who are willing can obtain a holder right for a fee.
In practice, the holder receives a guide for his 187-square-metre parcel, which means in practice that approval is not required from each holder if the purpose of use of the land is to be changed.
Holder rights can also be acquired for several blocks.
Acquiring the right of possession is something concrete that can be left as an inheritance or given as a gift.
You don't have to start carrying banners or chain yourself somewhere to protect nature," Juha Leviäkangas, who acquired the right of possession for his company, justifies.
Those who have acquired the right of possession will receive accurate location information about their block, which will enable, for example, a visit to the block.
On the Foundation's website, the name information of those who have acquired the holder rights is displayed on the same map where the parcel information on the land can be found.
Leviäkangas sees the only risk that the foundation's operations change from its original idea.
The Helsinki Foundation has been established as a non-profit foundation to promote the protection of the environment and nature.
The risk is that the foundation would somehow operate unethically or commercially, but it should not be possible.
The basic idea of Helsinki Foundation's Haltija project is the protection of natural areas so that they can be untouched and in peace.
The foundation procures land areas either by purchase or as a donation.
It finances its operations mainly through the sale of holder rights, but the foundation has received its start-up capital from its founders.
The purpose of the foundation is to acquire more protected countries.
It is also aiming beyond Finland's borders and is planning land acquisitions from Australia.
The Helsinki Foundation will present its activities in a pop-up forest at Helsinki Market Square from 13 to 19 August.
Bicycle thieves torment Turku the hardest among large cities - see the situation in your home municipality
Bicycle thieves torment Turku the hardest among Finland's major cities.
This year, there have been more bikes stolen relative to the number of inhabitants than in the nine other largest cities.
On the basis of the bicycle thefts reported to the police, Helsingin Sanomat investigated the number of bicycles and parts of bicycles and their parts per thousand inhabitants in the ten largest cities in January-August this year.
In addition to Turku, Helsinki, Oulu and Lahti took the lead.
According to police records, four to five bicycle thefts occurred per thousand inhabitants.
On the other hand, only about two bike thefts were reported to the police in Espoo and Kouvola per thousand inhabitants.
It's a big difference, isn't it?
"Relative to the number of inhabitants in some cities, double the number of thefts occurs compared to others," says Tommi Reen, Police Superintendent of the National Police Board.
The Turku police are wondering why the city is at the top of the comparison.
It is extremely difficult to say why more bikes have been stolen in Turku than elsewhere.
I don't have an answer for that.
"The prevalency of the theft can be influenced by how much people move around the city on bikes and how many bikes there are in the first place," says Sergeant Teemu Äikäs.
Tommi Reen of the National Police Board considers it quite expected that Turku, Helsinki and Oulu will rise to the top in the comparison.
The number of bicycle thefts goes largely according to the study and cycling cities.
Turku has a dense city center, short distances and a lot of bikes available for thieves.
"For example, Espoo is a completely different type of city, where urban centres are distant from each other, and a bicycle is not necessarily the primary means of transport," Reen says.
In the whole of Finland, slightly fewer bike thefts have been reported to the police in January-August than in the corresponding period last year.
However, there is no need for great joy.
Despite the downturn in recent years, the police become aware of just over 20 000 bike thefts each year.
That's 50-60 bikes every day and about 4 000 bikes a year in Helsinki alone.
"They're large quantities," Reen says.
In reality, thieves take more bikes, as only some of the victims report the theft to the police.
Reen urges people to report a bicycle theft, although fewer than one in ten cases reported to the police will be found out.
A typical solved bike theft is one in which the police are immediately informed of the theft and the thief gets caught in its blood.
"Cases can also be found in connection with other investigations, when, for example, stolen bikes can be found in the apartment in a narcotics case," Reen says.
In Helsinki, bike thieves are caught every week in connection with the act itself or in connection with a police investigation, says Senior Constable Timo Leppämäki.
According to him, bikes are stolen, for example, from shopping centre bike racks, courtyards, warehouses and bike cellars, i.e. practically everywhere where they are usually stored.
As in previous years, Jopoja is the most stolen from individual bike models.
The U-lock provides the bike with better protection than many other locks.
Some thieves take the bike, for example, for short-lived use, but a large number of stolen bikes end up for sale online, for example.
They remain on the domestic market.
"At least it has not come to the attention of the police that many stolen bikes would go abroad," Reen says.
According to the police, buyers of stolen bikes are ordinary Finns.
Some people will probably buy a stolen bike unknowingly.
Some may have doubts about it.
The buyer of the used bike must be careful, as he or she may be guilty of a concealment offence or offence.
"Alarming bells should ring if a bike in good condition is sold at a clear underprice," Reen says.
Severi Hirvi, a specialist librarian, carries his colourful Nopsa all the way during his working day to the shelters of the Kallio Library in Helsinki.
He's had five bikes, but not one has been stolen, Hirvi says. "It's never been, it's been a good monk."
"Maybe this doesn't tempt thieves so much, there isn't necessarily any hipster value in this either," Hirvi from Helsinki ponders.
Siiri Valve, who quickly visited a kiosk in Kallio, Helsinki, locked her bicycle with a small U-lock.
Now the bike stood against the kiosk wall, but Valve usually locks its bikes to something fixed, such as a bike rack.
"I'll think about it quite a lot, because this bicycle is the most expensive thing I own," says a student from Helsinki about his white bike, the frame of which alone cost about five hundred euros.
"All the time you hear from your friends that you've been taking bikes."
"Mostly bad bikes, sluggish-Jops."
A bicycle was stolen from the courtyard of the housing company a year ago.
The bike hadn't been locked, though, so Valve was waiting to seize it.
Children under the age of 15 responsible for their crimes?
Helsinki is experimenting with peer juries familiar from the United States
In Helsinki, a so-called peer jury is being tested to prevent young people from falling into a spiral of crime.
Initially, the experiment applies to people under the age of 15, who are now left without criminal liability.
A peer jury intervenes in the first crime committed by a young person.
The idea is for the peer jury to discuss what has happened with the perpetrator of the crime and impose a sanction.
A week later, the perpetrator of the crime can join the peer jury in order to make amends.
Other consequences may include, for example, a letter written to the family explaining what has been done and what has been learned from it.
Youth Director Tommi Laitio says that the model is not strict.
We have other conciliation models that are used in parallel.
"Everyone steers young people's actions in a more responsible direction so that everything would not be ruined by the first mistake," Laitio says.
The Peer-to-Peer Jury has been inspired by the United States, where it is widely used.
The idea is that after their first crime, the young person will not fall in front of an adult panel, but will reflect on what has happened with approximately the same age.
The model has been tested once.
Iikka Lovio, a service designer at Palmu's service design agency that followed the experiment, says that this was an assault, which was successfully investigated.
In particular, the first offences of under 15-year-olds may not have been dealt with at all before.
"It's been a message that you'll continue on your chosen path," Lovio says.
The Peer-to-peer jury is part of the Immigrant Youth Helsinki project, which is backed by the City of Helsinki and the Me Foundation.
Lovio emphasises that new practices are offered to all those in need, not just immigrant young people.
