From 2018 onwards, Olympic sports will be visible on both Yle and commercial channels.
How deep is Finland's deepest lake?
According to the official statistics, Finland's deepest lake is Päijänne, which has a depth of 95.3 metres in Ristinselkä.
It may be even deeper.
Technical diving trainer Matti Kannela found a 20-square-metre indentation in Ristiselkä, the depth of which the diving computer showed 104 metres.
In the summer of 2014, the Finnish Environment Institute unnecessarily tried to strengthen the observation by sonaring.
The method may not find a slight recess from a hundred meters, says Development Engineer Jari Hakala.
So far, the official depth is still 95.3 meters.
Other deep lakes are Lake Inari (92 metres) and Suvasvesi (90 metres).
More than 80 metres from Saimaa, Toisvesi and Pääjärvi.
They have not been studied as closely as Päijänne.
In particular, the bottom of Lake Inari has not been carefully mapped.
"There could well be unfamiliar depths," Kannela reflects.
If the deepest lake is defined by medium depth, it is the Great Juka Lake, 23 meters.
In this way, Päijänne does not make it to the top ten and Suvasvesi is even further away from the tip.
