The people of Japan prefer the Nordic countries to Finland - tourists are attracted by the Northern Lights: It is possible to see a fairy-tale mirror aurora in the autumn
The purpose of the campaign is to attract Japanese people to start Northern Lights trips already in the early autumn, Visit Finland says in a press release.
The main destinations of Japanese Northern Lights tourism are Inari and Levi.
The Northern Lights are the trump cards of our winter tourism, and they are strongly associated with the heart winter and snowy landscapes.
There is a lot of demand for Northern Lights trips in Lapland, but we are now trying to increase the sales of autumn trips.
Few people know that the Northern Lights season starts from early autumn and continues well into the spring.
In the autumn, it is possible to see the so-called mirror aurora, where the surface of the lake reflects the Northern Lights, making the sight double the fairy tale-like, says Anne Lind, Acting Marketing Manager at Visit Finland.
Visit Finland's Northern Lights campaign's main target group consists of the wealthy 25-35-year-old Japanese women living in the city, dreaming of tourism and new experiences.
They are already interested in Finland, its nature and design.
Tourists are lured to Finland, for example, through online advertising.
According to a survey carried out by researchers at Aalto University and the universities of Turku and Tokyo, Finland has an exceptionally strong and positive landscape among the Japanese compared to the other Nordic countries.
However, the Japanese would need clearer unique sights from Finland, and here the Northern Lights have huge potential, according to Lind.
Overnight stays by Japanese tourists in Finland have clearly increased more than in the other Nordic countries.
Last year, they spent more than twice as many overnight stays in Finland as in Sweden, Denmark or Norway.
From the point of view of Finnish tourism, the Japanese are an interesting target group, as they spent more than 80 million euros in Finland.
On average, the Japanese spent 183 euros a day in Finland, Lind says.
Britain had to settle for a silver medal in the rugby-7 race when Fidz's champion team won gold
Great Britain's impressive success in a seven-player rugby race was smashed when Fidzi defeated 43–7 in Thursday's final at Deodoro Stadium in Rio.
Fidzi taught the champions in ball handling, transfers, sigvents, support game, running lines and raw power and thus won gold – if there was the first Olympic medal for the country in its entire history.
The loss meant silver, which was a bit of a thin comfort for Britain, as it was the country's third silver on the same day. They can be proud of their success as an Olympic sport in the first-time rugby-7 tournament, in which the team defeated New Zealand, Argentina and South Africa.
A strict victory over the South African Boxes in the 7–5 semi-finals earlier in the same day could negatively affect the team in the finals.
Despite this, Fidz's team in this form would have beaten the Brits, even if the 15-person team would have been in their full strength.
It may bring comfort to Great Britain that at least one Briton will receive a gold medal tomorrow: Fidz head coach Ben Ryan, a former coach from Wimbledon for the English rugby-7.
The experience has been considerable for Great Britain.
Eight years ago, they lost all the games at the Adelaide Sevens tournament under Ryan's coaching.
In Rio, they lost only one game.
It's a loss, but it's startling.
Fidzi got off to an excellent start and showed off his tackling skills to the UK as if for fun.
It looked as if Captain Tom Mitchell and his team had collided with moving buses, and world champions took a 5–0 lead when Osea Kolinisau plunged into the corner.
After four minutes on the clock, Jerry Tuwai scored the second goal on the right.
Situation 12–0, and Great Britain had barely touched the ball.
Mitchell's superb tackle prevented Leone Nakarawa from scoringing a third goal, but it didn't slow Fidz's run for a long time.
After seven minutes of play, Jasa Veremalua scored on the right-handed corner, leaving Great Britain 17–0.
Nakarawa scored the second goal of overtaking, with more than a minute of playing time in the first half.
Vatemo Ravouvou then plunged through the British team's more powerless tackles and plunged across the finish line.
After a goal-kick, the opening period ended 29–0.
It seemed that the Fijians were born to play seven-player rugby, while the Britons are still in the role of an apprentice.
Which is largely true.
Britain made large-scale changes at half and managed to hold the Fidzis for a whole four minutes, which was a good performance compared to the first half, until Josua Tuisova scored thanks to an excellent team game for the Fijians.
Dan Norton managed to narrow the situation to 36–7 by scoringing for the United Kingdom on the left-hand corner, but the game had long since been losted to the South Sea Archipelagos, whose fans sang, waved flags and danced.
It was hard not to admire Fidz's purely joyful attitude.
Mata Viliame made the finishing touch with the Fijian cake by scoringing in the final moments.
Whistle rings, the Fijians embraced each other and the British fell on the field, completely crushed.
Earlier in the day, Fidzi had defeated Japa in the semi-finals by 20–5 and Great Britain had slipped past South Africa in a tight semi-final, where the tie was decided by Great Britain's goal kick.
Kyle Brown took South Africa's Boks to the lead in the first half, but Dan Norton crossed the finish line with his dazzling footwork and plunged from under the goal posts in the second half.
Marcus Watson, the brother of Anthony Watson, a player of England's 15-player rugby team, sealed the victory and eventually a silver medal.
Helen Glover and Heather Stanning were encouraged by the coach's fight against cancer
It couldn't be more emotional than gold in these competitions.
From the first bet to the last, Helen Glover and Heather Stanning rowed 220 triumphant bets into history and found an additional purpose for their golden moment in Estacio da Lagoa.
Even though they were in the oars, the boat echoed the voice of coach Robin Williams, who cheered them on to their second consecutive Olympic gold medal in the women's duo. This makes them the first women in the history of British sport to achieve a similar achievement.
Their superiority was undisputed, the victory for them was already the 39th in a row, and once again a record.
What we see, two brilliant athletes gliding on the surface of the water like swans of age does not fully correspond to the nature of success.
There is a large number of helping hands behind each rowing gold, each of which has contributed to the duo towards achievements. However, the most important of the assistants is the coach, who was diagnosed with cancer 30 months ago and didn't know then if he would see this day.
According to Williams, the situation was tough at the time.
"I had bladder cancer, and the tumor could be cut off.
If it hadn't been surgery, it would've spread everywhere
The situation would have been pretty bad.
It'll change the way of thinking."
Williams was diagnosed in December 2013.
The trio were lucky that Williams was able to start taking care of the sport during the season in early 2014, when the interruption caused as little inconvenience as possible, if you can even say this in such circumstances.
"I needed money, so I had to heal quickly.
It acted as an incentive.
I was in the hospital and I was walking around the ward for 6, 9 and 15 meters at a time when they said a few steps is enough.
That's where it starts.
Rowing itself is already a journey, but this made it a little bigger."
Immediately after the victory, the duo's thoughts were on Williams, who had spent a nervous morning and talked a little more than normal trying to win their own restlessness.
Captain Stanning of the Royal Artillery Regiment of the United Kingdom, who had a tour of Afghans behind him, says: "At the emotional level, I've been in ruins this week, which is perhaps a little extreme, but it means so much."
"It's just telling me how much this means to me and how much we've worked together with Helen and Robin.
For the past three years, Helen's and Robin's cash register has been fantastic.
If it wasn't for Robin, we wouldn't be anything, so it's a huge thank you to him, he's the best coach in the world.
“I couldn’t have hoped for a better person to work out with or a better coach.”
Helen Glover and Heather Stanning haven't losted any competition in the last five years.
The duo has found an overwhelming rhythm for which their rivals have not been able to compare.
