A tsunami from the past - People's News
The sea is fatal, but the actual tsunami is hitting the middle of Madrid's hustle and bustle - reckoning with the past can begin.
That's the essence of Pedro Almodóvar's novelty.
Almodovar's previous directing The Travel Lovers (2013) was a little funny and rough satirical comedy, preceded by Skin where I live (2011) the pinnacles of the Spanish master's career.
On a levely basis, Julieta (2016) is located somewhere between those two works.
It's a conventionally serene drama of love, relationships, and a family that focuses on the relationship between mother and daughter.
The story is simple and is largely seen as a long flashback.
Life seems to be on track for a middle-aged naming person (Emma Suarez), but then everything threatens to collapse.
In the aftermath of the occasional encounter, a new boyfriend (Dario Grandinetti) gets to be amazed all the way to the end of the film.
In the form of a flashback and a long letter, Julieta recounts the stages of her life to her adult daughter.
He begins the time before the birth of his daughter, the moments when he met his future father (Daniel Grao).
At the same the same time, Adraina Ugarte, who plays Julieta as a young teacher, will take the lead role in the second female lead, the TV-familiar series between Ommelte (2013-2014).
Julieta's aging has been taken care of in a way that can be considered crooky, but I'd say it's pretty quirky and literally with a one-time swipe.
For Almodóvar films, there is often a fair distance to such a characteristic sluggishness all the way to the humour.
It's an equally acted, discreet, place in places with good music and quite melancholy film, in which it has elements of elegy.
On the other hand, the Spanish director's characteristic visual saturation is still there and in such a way that it gradually begins to catch the eye more as a pure formality than as a functional dramatic means of efficiency.
The characters in the film are affected by illnesses, deaths or otherwise just sudden losses, but the surface remains just as fine.
This is especially true for costumes: in the case of the naming person, the outfits change from scene to scene, and the same applies to spectacular earrings.
Almodóvar is precise in these matters, but the main point, i.e. the tweaks of the current scene, does not particularly improve.
When repeated mechanically, the impression is external and a little lifeless.
Almodóvar has written a manuscript on the basis of three short short stories by Alice Munro, a Canadian award-winning Nobel Prize for Literature.
The short stories have been published in Karkulainen: Stories.
Almodóvar has invested in dialogue, resulting in a somewhat even-thick literary literature.
There would have been elements for an emotionally incomparably sturdier drama, so many big things - especially gnawing guilt - the theme of the film deals with.
Written and directed by Pedro Almodóvar.
Based on Alice Munro's three short stories.
Starring: Emma Suarez, Adriana Ugarte, Rossy de Palma, Daniel Grao, Dario Grandinetti.
The premiere of the film is 12.8.
The government is trying the election - Kansan News
There will be no new cuts, the ministers say so that the Finns Party will not completely collapse.
However, the cuts decided in the spring will be carried out.
There won't be any new cuts.
The four-billion savings already decided are enough.
Prime Minister Juha Sipilä (Centre Party) and Petteri Orpo (National Coalition Party), Minister of Financily (National Coalition Party).
This is true in the sense that there are no new cuts in the Ministry of Finance's proposal, as social security cuts were already decided during the spring spending limits session.
Child benefit, national pension, basic unemployment security and almost all other benefits linked to the national pension index will be cut by 0.85 per cent.
The duration of earnings-related unemployment security will be shortened from 500 days to 400.
Education cuts continue.
Municipal elections haunt
Why do ministers stress so much that there will be no new cuts on them?
Because next year is an election year.
Municipal elections will be held in April and they are the most important thermometer of the parliamentary term, not polls.
It has been seen before that the municipal election result, which takes the government parties in different directions, ruins the mood of the government for a long time.
However, according to the polls, the Finns Party expects a defeat, even though the result of its previous elections was a modest 12.3 per cent compared to the parliamentary elections.
Four years ago, the Finns Party received 752 new councillors, a large number of whom will realize the price of influencing in the right-wing government next spring.
The lifeline of the governments of about three equal parties depends on the Finns Party's strength to withstand.
Now it hasn't lasted, but more than halves of the party's voters got enough of government policy already during the first year.
The first signs of the party's parliamentary group have also surfaced.
First of all, the rhetoric about new curcips is obscuring the fact that next year they will be made drastically and, secondly, attempts will be made to soften the attack that awaits the Finns Party.
Major cuts in 2018?
After a year, everything will probably be different - if Sipilä's government is still standing.
The Government has set itself the goal of indebtedness during this parliamentary term, but Petteri Orpo's budget proposal will take on 5.9 billion euros next year, i.e. more than this year.
After the municipal elections, the government has two options.
The first is to raise its hands already in the middle of the parliamentary term and admits that the government will not be able to follow its own programme: indebtedness does not bend or even slow down.
The second is to stick to the program.
On top of the past, this means new even tougher cuts in the 2017 budget session.
The budget would at least come close to balance during 2018, and then in the spring we will accept what will happen in the parliamentary elections.
With these measures, indebtedness does not work
It is quite clear that with the current measures the indebtedness will not work, although Juha Sipilä is trying to incite a positive mood.
The curves presented by State Secretary Martti Hetemäki at the press conference on the budget prove it irrefutably:
Growth forecasts for the global economy have repeatedly turned out to be over-optimistic, i.e. there will be no traction from the international economy.
Finland's national economy has not risen much since the collapse experienced in 2008, except temporarily during the stimulus policy pursued in the early 2010s.
Unemployment has increased by 35 per cent during this decade and has protracted on average from 34 to 54 weeks.
Unemployment now costs three billion euros a year more than in 2008.
With Hetemäki's presented curves, general government debt, which exceeded the 60 per cent threshold in 2014, will rise to 70 per cent in the middle of the next decade.
What is responsible?
In the spring of 2015, the debt stimulus presented by the Left Alliance was rejected because it was considered irresponsible.
Next year, the maintenance of unemployment will cost about six billion euros, which will practically be covered by taking on a completely new debt.
This is the view of the Centre Party, the National Coalition Party and the Finns Party about what is responsible.
