Starting as a rural entrepreneur meant borrowing and an increase in the size of the farm so that the bread could come from agriculture.
It's been my thing since I was a little boy.
I've always been happy to work on the farm, and now that I've started as an entrepreneur myself, I've put everything on the line.
If there's anything to start, I think you'll have to do it seriously and seriously.
In 2013, we set out to build a barn, and at first the bank wasn't enthusiastic about it either.
However, I was really brave enough to get involved in the project and I've been fully involved in it, Salomon Koli says and adds that he wouldn't have taken action if he didn't have the faith in producing clean Finnish food and that everything would turn out too good.
Salomon Koli, which breeds meat trucks, aims to invest in an even bigger barn.
The work has paid off, as Salomon Koli has been selected in the top ten in Maaseudun Future magazine's young food producer competition.
He is currently competing for a possible victory.
You can vote for him on the magazine's website until 21.8.
For the first time, Bulgaria will report inflation in eight months
According to national statistics, Bulgaria's monthly inflation was 1% in July.
This is the highest value in many years (since June 2012) and the first time it rises above zero since October of last year, when the countrey reported positive inflation of 0.2 per cent.
The average annual inflation measured by the CPI over the past 12 months (August 2015 to July 2016) was –0.8% compared to the previous 12 months (August 2014 to July 2015).
Compared to June 2016, the Consumer Price Index was 101.0% in July 2016, i.e. monthly inflation was 1.0%.
Since the beginning of the year (July 2016, the percentage of inflation has been –0.2% compared to December 2015), and the annual inflation rate was –0.2% in July 2016.
Compared to the previous month in July 2016, there was an increase in the prices of the following consumable groups: food and non-alcoholic beverages (1.7%), vehicles (1.8%), recreation and culture (6.3%), restaurants and hotels (1.2%), other goods and services (0.3%).
Prices fell in clothing and footwear (2.2%), furniture, household appliances and scheduled maintenance (0.5%) and health (0.1%).
The prices of the following product groups remained at the same level as in the previous month: alcoholic beverages and tobacco, housing, water, electricity, gas and other fuels, communication and training.
Snapchat's anime effect criticized as Asian caricature
Snapchat says the filter was inspired by the anime, but some observers think it's racist.
Snapchat's lens tool allows approximately 150 million daily users to change reality and play with identity in ways that converge absurdity.
You can turn yourself into pineapple, a dog, or a figure in Roy Lichtenstein's painting.
Lentils are unadorned, feature-falsifying tools to create more than 30 million intensified selfies per day.
All missteps quickly become public knowledge.
Snapchat's lenses have been criticized, where it has been accused of favoring blackface makeup or bleached skin tones as a beauty ideal.
So when it offered some of its users a filter this week that turned their eyes crooked, twisted their teeth, and turned their cheeks chubby, some of the critics called it a racist caricature of Asian people.
Critics also wondered whether these recurring contradictions referred to the greater problem that the company has with diversity.
The news and rage spread extensively on Wednesday and was written about, among other things, by the online publications The Verge and Motherboard the day after Snapchat announced that it had removed the filter.
The company gave the following explanation: the lens was intended as a tribute to the anime characters, not the caricature of Asian people.
But for observers who have experienced racism, the lens was a reminder of offensive stereotypes.
Some vehemently disputed the anime parable.
Grace Sparapani, a Korean-American art student whose tweet was widely shared, said in an e-mail that the lens was "at least offensive and embarrassing."
He adds: "It's hard to deny equating a very tasteless Asian caricature with filter effects.
It shows that the filter is not only a "yellowface", but one that has been taken to derogatory extreme."
Snapchat isn't the only company that has to avoid these cultural traps.
In American culture, there seems to be an endless struggle for diversity and social inclusion, from corporate boards to Hollywood and the devices we carry with us.
Snapchat's large audience is made up of young people who have a more diverse ethnic background than their older counterparts. This may mean that they're likely to look forward to discreet.
"When one of Snapchat's lenses creates a picture that offends the user, these types of things don't go unnoticed very easily anymore, as it did before," 25-year-old Katie Zhu said in an interview on Thursday.
Zhu, who works as a product manager and designer on the Medium publishing platform, decided on Thursday to delete his Snapchat account and encouraged others to do the same.
In a post published on Medium and a phone interview, he said he believed ethnic contradictions reflected a lack of appreciation of diversity in Snapchat's recruitment practices.
Zhu criticized the company's leadership, which consists mostly of whites and men alone, and ended his writing with a hashtag: #DeleteSnapchat.
"It's either that there aren't people of color on their one-sided staff, in which case they can make decisions like this, or they also have people in color as employees, but their position isn't such that they'll have the courage to express their opinions directly and easily," says Chinese-American Zhu.
Other observers agree with him, but Snapchat has largely failed to respond to the claim.
The company does not publish figures on the ethnic background of its staff, citing its status as a private company.
On Thursday, Snapchat declined to discuss the ethnic background of its staff, but according to its spokesperson, the company has recently hired a recruiter to focus on under-represented groups of people and promoting inclusion within the company.
For his part, Zhu says he'll keep his Snapchat account closed and add, "I wonder if they really need more users like us in order to say it's not okay."
According to an expert group on Congress, military officials misrepresented intelligence on ISIS
On Thursday, a group of military experts released a report, according to a more optimistic picture of the war against ISIS in Iraq and Syria than what the events on the ground suggested.
The Republican presidents of the U.S. House of Representatives (House Armed Services Committee) Intelligence and Defense Appropriation Set up a working group in an interim report about the "commonal dissatisfaction" of military intelligence analysts. According to analysts, senior military officials embellished their assessments of Americans' actions against ISIS.
The Central Command, in short Centcom, is the U.S. Armed Forces headquarters in Tampa, Florida, responsible for military operations in the Middle East and Central Asia.
"Centcom's senior executives generally gave a more positive picture of U.S. anti-terrorism actions than what the facts on the field showed, and were invariably more positive than the intelligence community's analysis of other entities," the report said in a press release.
"Centcom's events are unacceptable – our soldiers are suffering when high-level political decision-makers are presented with false analyses," California Republican spokeswoman Ken Calvert said.
"Centcom's management errors extend to the highest level of the organization."
Extensively 10-page, the report details the persistent problems encountered in 2014 and 2015, the descriptions of Central Command and the analysis of Americans' efforts to train Iraqi troops.
Although the report does not contain any conclusive evidence that reports commissioned by Obama's governing authorities were forged, it gives a picture of analysts who give the impression that Centcom's directors have pressured them to present a more optimistic view of the threat posed by ISIS.
"In the first half of 2015, many of Central Command's press releases, statements, and congressional witness statements were significantly more positive than the events actually were," the report said.
"For example, a Centcom official made a public statement that a major military trush to retake Mosul could take place as early as April or May 2015."
Mosul, Iraq's second-largest city, is still under ISIS control.
"After months of research, this is very clear," Kansas Republican MP Mike Pompeo said in a statement.
"From mid-2014 to mid-2015, U.S. Armed Forces' supreme intelligence executives manipulated obtained intelligence results to belittle ISIS' threat in Iraq."
Republicans set up a working group after learning of the suspicions expressed by analysts that intelligence about ISIS had been manipulated.
The report, which was published on Thursday, is followed by more detailed results, as investigations are still ongoing.
In addition, the Ministry of Defence's superintendent has launched an investigation into Centcom's intelligence.
On Thursday, Democrat members of the House Intelligence Committee released their own results, which were partly in line with the conclusions of the Republican Working Group.
"Between 2014 and 2015, Centcom created an excessively limited process to assess intelligence on ISIS and Iraqi security forces," said Adam B. Schiff, the committee's high-ranking Democrat.
According to Schiff, this process "delayed the publication of the intelligence results, "offended the morality of the analysts" and "takes insufficient attention to differing views."
According to Schiff and Democrats, however, there was no evidence that the White House was trying to pressure Centcom's analysts to reconcile its conclusions with a "predetermined or political official line."
The Daily Beast reported on the preliminary results of a congressional inquiry on Tuesday.
Last August, the New York Times briefed the Pentagon Superintendent investigation, which began as a result of actions filed by Centcom Analysts.
Pentagon spokesman and lieutenant commander Patrick Evans said in a statement that the Department of Defense would not comment on the congressional report as long as an investigation into the inspector general is ongoing.
However, he says: "Experts sometimes disagree on the interpretation of complex information, and the intelligence community and the Department of Defense welcome a healthy dialogue on these key national security issues."
In addition to trying to find out, U.S.-led bombings in Iraq and Syria have been a success (a claim made by an increasing number of defence authorities).
However, last year's claims questioned how much President Obama can rely on the Central Command and giving honest assessments of a military operation in Iraq, Afghanistan, Libya and other crisis areas.
Criticism of ISIS intelligence is in line with disagreements that emerged more than a decade ago, according to a study by Centcom's intelligence analyst Gregory Hooker, according to a study by President George W. Bush's government, despite the objections of many analysts, supported only small troops in Irak and spent little time thinking about what would result from the 2003 invasion.
Hooker also played a central role in the revolution against ISIS intelligence.
According to the authorities, Last year, Hooker's working group came to the conclusion, according to opposing public statements, according to contradictory public statements, air strikes on refineries controlled by ISIS had not significantly weakened the economy of the terrorist ground because it had built temporary refineries and was thus able to sell oil on the black market.
However, the result of the study was not mediated outside the Central Command, The Times reported in September.
Centcom's then-commander, General Lloyd J. Austin III, was criticized last year after many legislators judged that the witness statements given to the Senate were too positive estimates of the war.
General Austin retired in April and was replaced by General Joseph L. Votel as commander of the Central Command.
The United States wins the fourth duel in the women's field hockey and establishs itself in a showdown with Britain
After scoring three goals in a victorious 6–1 game against Japan, Katie Bam scored the first two goals for the U.S. women's squat hockey team on Thursday, when the United States defeated India.
Five goals in two games could be considered a great achievement, but the 27-year-old Bam wasn't sure if he'd done the same before.
"I don't have a clue," he says.
"I don't belong to the people who keep track of the goals they've scored."
However, that's what he knows: The Americans have won four games in their Olympic tournament and lost zero games and share first place in their group with Britain, who continued their winning streak by defeating Japan 2–0 on Thursday.
Britain and the United States will play against each other on Saturday in the final game of the first round.
Both teams made it to the final, which determines which of them is the first in the six-team group and which is second.
The first to finish will avoid a long-term encounter with the tough Dutch team that won the second group.
Regardless of what's going to happen in Saturday's game, the Americans have already sparked a controversy about their surprising success in these Games, four years after finishing last from the 12 teams at the London Olympics.
Bam was also on the team in London.
He grew up at Blue Bell in the state of Pennsylvania and grabbed a field hockey racket for the first time at the age of 3 (his older two-sisters played sports in Drexel). He joined the national team at the age of 16 and was the youngestest national team player of all time.
He missed the 2008 Olympics when he didn't make it to the final list of players.
After this came the 2012 Games, where he and his team hardly saw any successes.
The following year, Craig Parnham of England was appointed coach, after which things began to change.
"Change was cultural, spiritual and physical," says Bam.
"Our pace of work in London wasn't what it's now.
“We’ve made a complete turn.”
On Thursday night, Bam opened a goal account in the first half by hitting the ball close with a backhand.
India had entered the game without a single tournament victory, but it turned out to be a surprisingly tough opponent and retained a 1–0 situation all the way to the third period, when Bam stepped onto the pitch.
Bam transported the ball for a long time along the left side of the pitch, squirmed into the midfield, hit the ball into the goal and thus took the Americans into a 2–0 lead.
Melissa Gonzalez scored the last goal in the middle of the fourth period.
The game was the first in the tournament, where U.S. goaltender Jackie Briggs managed to keep the goal empty. In the first three games, one hit had been scored by the Americans.
Briggs is now in his successful season,
Just like Bam.
"Our ranking is now good," says Parnham, but how good it depends on the outcome of Saturday's game.
In the middle of the Olympic hulina trampoline brings zen
Regardless of place and time, you will inevitably come across the Olympics.
The events, competitions and struggles in Rio de Janeiro seem to follow as a frenzied message all the time and everywhere.
It's as if the Olympic fire itself were a flaming relay baton moving from the archer to the swimmer, from the swimmer to the gymnast and from the gymnast to the triathlonist.
You can't open a TV, read a newspaper, or listen to your neighbor's conversation in the café's queue, you'll necessarily hear about Simone Biles, Katie Ledecky, or a quarrel between Michael Phelps and Chad le Clos.
And then there's a man named Logan Dooley.
Dooley, a first-timer of the Olympics, charms with his smile and might well be a name on everyone's lips that could be seen and heard in the prime viewing and listening time, would be favored by the sponsors, and would appear in feel-good TV commercials if he were just a swimmer, a gymnast, or even a fencer.
But Dooley, 28, from Lake Forest, California, competes for Olympic gold on the trampoline.
The Olympic fire does not illuminate all competitions equally.
Farther from the center, partly in the shade are lesser-known sports, such as air pistol shooting, archery, badminton and taekwondo.
We're always going to have a breaststroke, and let's stay the championship with us for a long time.
But if I can write a modest article about my special favorite, I want to draw attention to the restrained charm of the trampoline.
Although the trampoline has been an Olympic sport since the 2000 Sydney Games, its visibility for the last 16 years has been so limited that many of the people I interviewed did not even know it was an Olympic sport.
Ignorance can be region-specific.
From the United States, athletes have participated in the Olympic trampoline, but the country has never made medals and has not progressed to the finals until 2012.
In the men's trampoline, the medals seem to go to the Chinese and Russians, and on the women's side the medal catch have been grabbed by the Canadians.
China's Dong Dong and Canada's Rosie MacLennan defend gold medals this year, which they won in London.
However, the trampoline has the charm of the underdog, partly because it sounds more suitable for a circus school than for the Olympics.
(In this context, I acknowledge that I am not an expert, only an ordinary fan.)
The sport has its comic potential, which has been used in cartoon movies indefinitely in the characters' constant bouncing scenes. But it also has the same dignity as gymnastics and diving, i.e. sports that are essentially connected on the trampoline.
The word trampoline comes from the Spanish word el trampolín, which means "spruggle board".
Ambitious masters jump on a huge trampoline that throws them at an altitude of almost 10 meters. Before landing on their feet, jumpers have to do a series of acrobatic movements, such as twists, folds and volts.
According to the official Olympic programme, the jumps are graded based on performance, difficulty and "flight time".
In Rio, there are 32 athletes competing for the trampoline gymnastics championship, who come from 17 different countries, of whom there are 16 men and 16 women.
The most common direction of Olympic sports is straight ahead.
In pursuit of gold, medal hopes need to go further, faster, up and above, sometimes across the finish line, sometimes back and forth around the field.
In addition to the finish line or the starting wall, they also have another goal that they aspire to in televised reports, namely the world or Olympic record.
This determined pursuit is noble, admirable, even fanatical – and is even enough to bring the viewer into tears.
But I also find this a bit exhausting only when watching the races.
This trampoline offers a respite.
In the trampoline, the direction is upwards.
The goal is not to move forward.
Gymnast bounces up weightless like an astronaut, falls down and effortlessly swings back up.
Of course, the sport also includes a competitive aspect, and it's not easy either, but it doesn't involve an intense rush to the finish line or jump far, just a dreamy and smooth combination of time and gravity.
It's as close to Zen as it's possible to get to the Olympics.
Women's qualifiers and finals will take place on Friday afternoon and will start at 13:00 local time and the men's matches on Saturday at the same time.
NBC will broadcast the finals as part of its afternoon broadcast, which will start at 14:00 local time on both days.
In the middle of the Olympic mania bring a peaceful and wonderful moment that might go unnoticed in the middle of the general hustle and bustle.
And if consciousness can only be raised by offering male athletes to watch and objectifying them—according to U.S. male gymnasts, according to the Wall Street Jury recently suggested—then Logan Dooley is just looking forward to its panting new group of fans.
An entire generation protests in Ethiopia, a long-term ally of the United States
Violent demonstrations in Ethiopia
Government forces have forcibly dismassed demonstrations demanding political change across Ethiopia.
According to eyewitnesses, several demonstrators have been shot dead in clashes with police.
Is Ethiopia falling apart?
For the past decade, it has been one of Africa's stablest countries and a trustworthiness ally of the West, whose economy has grown rapidly.
Over the past few months, however, the country has been shaken by anti-government demonstrations, which are spreading to increasingly larger areas.
Last week alone, thousands of people stormed the streets demanding a fundamental political change.
According to human rights organizations, the government's reaction was ruthless
According to eyewitnesses, several unarmed demonstrators were shot and killed.
Of the demonstrations, supposedly filmed at the end of last year or the beginning of this year, the videos show security forces beating young people who are forced to stand with their hands against the wall.
The UN's chief human rights official is now calling for in-depth research.
"It has always been difficult to keep the country united, and it has always been more difficult to move forward," says Rashid Abdi, Research Director of the International Crisis Group of the African Sarvi project.
Ethiopia's population is Africa's second-largest country in terms of population.
The U.S. military and the intelligence service strongly support Ethiopians in the fight against terrorist threats throughout the region, especially in Somalia. Only a few African countries, if anything, get this much help from the West.
Ethiopia's economy has grown rapidly.
Its infrastructure has radically improved, the capital Addis Ababa even has a new conurbation train.
The streets of cities are usually peaceful, safe and clean.
However, Ethiopia is still a long way from the model country of democracy. Human rights organisations have constantly reported on the government's repressive policy, the opposition in the country is very limited and dissidents are quickly silenced.
Many people have been deported from the country, imprisoned, killed or driven far into the wilderness.
But the situation may be changing.
"If the people are suppressed and have no choice but to demonstrate, it will erupt," says Seyoum Teshome, a university lecturer in Central Ethiopia.
"The whole youth is demonstrating.
A whole generation protested."
There are many topics of dissatisfaction, ranging from land use to the government coality's stranglehold on power.
After last year's widely criticized elections, the ruling party and its allies won the last place that previously belonged to the opposition, as a result of which they now dominate the entire parliament.
At the same time, tensions are increasing on the border of Eritrea, where the escalating situation required hundreds of fatalities during the battle in June.
Analysts fear that in recent years, more or less defeated separatist groups, such as the Oromo Liberation Front or the Ogaden People's Liberation Front, may seek to exploit turmoil and re-arming.
There are many reasons why the embittered mood that has been smouldering under the surface for years is erupting right now has many reasons.
The first is apparently innocent: smartphones.
It wasn't until the last couple of years that Ethiopians have been able to communicate on social media, as cheaper smartphones became common and internet availability improved.
Even when the government repeatedly blocks access to Facebook and Twitter, especially during the demonstrations, many can access the Internet through proxies that encrypt their whereabouts.
Many Ethiopian young people said that this is how they invited people to the demonstrations.
Another reason is an increase in solidarity between Ethiopia's two largest population groups, Oromas and Amharos.
Oromos and Amharas are not natural allies.
For a long time, Amharas from Ethiopia's predominantly Christian highlands have ruled the country's politics and business and deprived Oromas, most of whom are Muslims and live in the Netherlands.
But this is also changing.
"The cooperation is deepening and we are about to get under the same umbrella," says Mulatu Gemechu, the oromo leader.
The biggest demonstrations have been in the Oromo and Amhara regions.
Many Amharas and Oromos feel that Ethiopia is unfairly ruled by Tigre's tribe, which represents only about six percent of the population and, however, dominates the army, intelligence services, commerce and politics.
The third cause of the unrest is Meles Zenaw's death.
Former Rebel Leader Meles was the prime minister of Ethiopia for 17 years until he died of an unnamed illness in 2012.
He was considered a tactical genius who could see around the corner.
According to the analysts, he was particularly skilled at observing the first signs of discontent and of using ambassadors who mediated between opponents.
"The current government lacks this essential instinct," says Conflict Investigator Abdi.
Ethiopia's new Prime Minister Hailemariam Desalegn stepped into Melesi's shoes almost out of famility.
Unlike Meles, who was from the Tigre region of northern Ethiopia, Hailemariam comes from the south.
According to the analysts, he does not have the confidence of the security forces managed by the Tigre tribe.
Many are afraid that this will mean more bloodshed.
The last time Ethiopia experienced similar riots in 2005, when thousands protested against the elections, which, according to analysts, were first screwed up and then stealed.
In post-electoral unrest, many protesters were killed, albeit with fewer deaths than in the previous months, and unrest ceased relatively quickly.
Development specialists have praised Ethiopia's leaders on far-sighted infrastructure planning, such as a new agglomeration train, and major advances in the fight against poverty.
But the internal resentment towards the Ethiopian government has clearly not stopped the intensification.
And a grudgefence is taking a dangerous ethnic form.
Last montumer in Gondari, the city of Amhara, protesters invaded companies owned by tigres, and anti-tigre hate speech is becoming more common on social media.
According to analysts, the protests put the United States and other Western allies in an awkward situation.
The U.S. government has used Ethiopia as a base for unmanned aircraft that monitored neighboring Somalia, but, according to the United States, the base has recently been closed.
At the same time that the West clearly wants to support democracy, it also does not want its already unstable alliance to collapse.
"This requires strict balancing," says Abdi.
Donald Trump says he was sarcastic by claiming Obama was the founder of ISIS
On Friday, Donald J. Trump attempted to revoke his words in a controversy over his latest campaign, claiming that he wasn't serious about claiming on several occasions this week as the "founders" of the terrorist group ISIS.
Clarification raised new questions for the Republican candidate's ability to communicate clearly to the citizens of their country.
His supporters, who have to try to defend their candidate in these turbulent moments, will also find themselves in an awkward situation and explain comments that he may not have meant.
Trump made the claim at Wednesday's election and reiterated in various interviews on Thursday that he really meant what he said when he claimed the president and Hillary Clinton created ISIS.
Early Friday morning, however, Trump tweeted and said his claim was sarcasm.
In the tweet, Trump swirled his speech from the day before, when he said in several interviews that he was quite serious about his accusations.
While radio host and Conservative Hugh Hewitt tried to help Trump to soften his claim that Obama was trying to destroy ISIS, Republican presidential nominee refused to back down.
The latest storm of reviews rose when Trump tried to centralize his campaign back into the economy as the numbers fell.
Trump and Clinton gave an economic policy speech in Michigan this week.
On Friday, Trump tried to come back.
He warned on Twitter that Clinton's proposals would raise taxes, and found Clinton's speech boring.
Trump has defended himself with sarcasm before.
At the end of July, he received widespread criticism when he appeared to urge the Russians to hacke Hillary Clinton's e-mail. He later explained his comments by saying, "Of course I was sarcastic."
In this way, Trump stretches the boundaries of political speech and draws his words back if necessary.
“I think it’s his way of testing certain messages that evoke a response to his adherents. He watches how supporters respond to messages, and then ‘clarifies’ in one way or another, depending on his reactions,” says communications expert Ruth Sherman, who’s been observing the language used by both presidential candidates.
"But I also believe that he understands his assertions and knows that they are vague enough to permeate different sieves."
Rather than Trump softened his statements to the election audience, he's in the last few days changed his style even more pungent as his approval ratings have continued to decline.
A new poll conducted by NBC, the Wall Street Journal and Marist released on Friday said Trump's support is significantly lower than Clinton's in North Carolina, Virginia and Colorado, while in Florida the situation is tightly even.
At the same time Trump usually tries to avoid apologizing for his provocative speeches, he often accuses his critics of misinterpreting his words or not understanding his sense of humour.
However, in the last few months of his presidential campaign, Trump's jokes have put his loyal supporters in an embarrassing status where they have to publicly defend Trump's comments no matter how provocative they may be.
One of Trump's most prominent supporters, the former mayor of New York, Rudolph W. Giuliani, strongly defended the presidential nominee in an interview with CNN on Thursday.
‘I think what he said was justified, a political speech,’ replied Giuliani, when asked if Trump was truthful in claiming Obama and Hillary Clinton had founded ISIS.
“It’s true in the sense that before Obama, ISIS was almost unknown, a small organization that Obama mistakenly called “the lower league team” and that’s why this happened because he withdrew the troops from Iraq.”
Most mainstream Republicans are trying to argue that the Obama administration, in which Hillary Clinton was foreign secretary, allowed ISIS to continue its activities when it did not determinately handled the situation in the Middle East.
Prior to Friday's election rallies in Pennsylvania, Trump continued his attack on the media on Twitter, where he blamed them for dwelling on his speeches and a discriminatory attitude towards him.
He mocked "unworthy and pathetic" television commenters who are trying to find out about him, but he said they can't do it.
Some Democrats mocked Trump's explanation, saying it would just show more clearly that he wasn't eligible for the presidency.
Due to the rise in unreasonable rent, a café opened in Greenpoint
Verb Cafe was a bit shabby café in Williamsburg, where only cash was accepted and which didn't have Wi-Fi, but which offered good coffee for 15 years.
Naturally, the café was priced out of its street-site on Bedford Avenue when the rent became unreasonable.
Soap Cherie, which replaced Verb Cafe in the summer of 2014, sells soaps shaped like cupcakes.
The locals find the loss of the café dodged from under the middle-class to be regrettable.
Artist Molly Crabapple tweeted: "New York is changing, I know, but I still can't believe the CBGB club doesn't exist anymore, the Chelsea Hotel has been destroyed and Verb is now a soap store."
But even if New York isn't what it used to be, they're also creating something new.
Last November, Verb Cafe opened a new café in Greenpoint, just over a kilometre north of its original location.
The new Verb Café has Wi-Fi and you can pay with a credit card, but some of the employees are the same and the atmosphere is still unpretentious.
The coffee is still good, too.
The owner and manager of the new Verb Café is Cisco Rodriguez, a long-time employee of the old café.
He started in Verb in 2001 and worked there until the end.
When a customer offered to finance the opening of a new café, he said he was very interested in doing it.
Rodriguez, 36, kept many things the same.
Verb orders bagels from the same supplier as before and uses the same espresso machine model.
In addition, it reintroduced even the lamps of the old Verb.
Rodriguez says, however, that he also wanted to try something new and
added new products to the list.
Bacon-egg-cheese-sandwiches are served with capers and browned onion rings.
There are also dark rice bowls on the list.
The café no longer sells beer, but juices made from fresh fruits instead.
After nine months running the café, Rodriguez said he noticed that old regular customers had started to visit the Verb for a steady stream.
"Many of our old customers were also priced out of Williamsburg," he says.
One of these is Rebecca Oliveira, who visited the café recently on Sunday.
Oliveira, 34, used to live in Williamsburg and regularly visited the Verb.
After moving to Greenpoint, he made walks to the Verb in Williamsburg at that time.
When Verb was shut down, he says he stopped drinking coffee for a while.
"I realized that I like Verbi's coffee and culture," he says.
Now he's drinking coffee again, even though on that Sunday he took a chai tea mix instead of his usual iced coffee, which is also a list novelty.
Another long-term customer, Tom Rosenthal, faithfully visited Verb since 2003.
He always went to the café before work and invariably ordered the same.
"I entered the café every day for a quarter of an hour, like a machine, and there they already prepared my butter bagel with all the fillings," he says.
Photo: Rodriguez also wanted to try something new and brought new products to the menu.
Today, Rosenthal still orders his standard product, but from time to time he varies his bagel bacon, egg and cheese fillings.
Rodriguez says there's no grudgefuse that the soap store took the old place.
Soap Cherie's owners even offered to make a special soap made using Verbi's own cold-braised coffee.
"We may still seize the offer," Rodriguez says.
Poles, who traditionally lived in Greenpoint, have also welcomed the café with joy.
One of the new customers turned the menu into Polish, and Rodriguez is now looking for a suitable place so that his new neighbors will feel welcome.
The new place has brought other benefits.
The local juice company wants to bottle and sell the café's cold brewed coffee.
The café may also expand into the adjacent building.
However, for many customers, it is enough for them to get their own old favourites from the new Verb.
Joseph Whitt was a regular in Williamsburg, and he says his heart was broken when the café was shut down.
When the new place was opened and I ordered a coffee there, it felt like to have come home, says Whitt.
"I needed the smell of the café."
Dready Food of the Great Recession in New Work: A Square Meal
In March 1933, shortly after becoming president, Franklin Delano Roosevelt sat down for lunch in his office in the White House.
Known as a culinarist, President Roosevelt was pleased with the luxurious dishes of Fifth Avenue's restaurants, such as foie gress pastry and Maryland turtle soup.
On that day, his menu was more modest: devil's eggs (deviled eggs) in tomato sauce, mashed potatoes, and plum pudding for dessert.
"It was a demonstration of culinary solidarity for the people who suffered," says Jane Ziegelman.
Her husband Andrew Coe adds: "It was also a message to Americans about how they should eat."
The Brooklyn Heights-based couple both work as food historians.
Coe's latest book, Chop Suey, tells about Chinese cuisine in America, while Ziegelman's most recent book, titled 97 Orchard, tells the story of an apartment building in the Lower East Side neighborhood through food.
Their new joint work is A Square Meal, published by Harper on Tuesday, which depicts the history of American food during the Great Recession of the 1930's.
The couple showed their own culinary solidarity by meeting with a reporter at the Eisenberg's Sandwich Shop, a small and ordinary lunch bar near Flatiron skyscraper and opened in the same year as the big stock market crash occurred, 1929.
Ziegelman, 54, ordered a cream cheese and olive-filled sandwich, while 57-year-old Coe ordered turkeys, mashed potatoes and mixed vegetables.
When a reporter ordered meatloaf, the couple said it was well-suited for a discussion about eating during the Great Recession.
"Loafs were very popular," says Jane Ziegelman.
"People ate peanut loaf, liver loaf and bean loaf.
They were made from substances that were inexpensive and sufficient.
Imagine eatinging so many peanuts that it's enough for the whole meal."
Andrew Coe's grimacing.
"It must've been pressed in the belly like lead," he says.
Before the Great Recession, the American dining table, especially in the countryside, operated on a buffet basis "eat as much as you want".
The women cooked food for the agricultural workers, and freshly baked pies were served for breakfast, lunch and dinner.
It was the Americans who had sent food to starving Europeans during the First World War. A Square Meal is a chronicle of the ways in which the people survived when abundance suddenly had to be renounced.
"This was a time when food became a central and distressing topic for the Americans," says Coe, explaininging why he and his wife wanted to write about it.
Equally distressing was the new inexpensive diet: according to Ziegelman, mysterious dishes such as meatloaves and stews became the main food, and stew dishes were said to be "a great way to take advantage of food scraps", because chefs may have masked not so tempting ingredients with cream sauce.
Fresh fruits were replaced with cheaper dried fruits.
Muscle, which for years had been the most central part of the American meal, became sparingly used luxury.
We experimented with strange mixtures, where the most important thing was the fulfilment of calorie and nutritional values, rather than the taste or even common kitchen sense.
While conducting research for the book, which also includes recipes, Jane Ziegelman prepared peanut butter-filled onion onions, which were used as a displacement.
"It didn't get any popularity at the dinner table," says Coe
Ziegelman adds: "It was unreal.
Peanut butter and oven onions don't go together at all.
This was characteristic of many household recipes."
Between 1923 and 1943, Louise Stanley, the head of the Federal Household Agency, guided citizens' eating habits in an unprecedented and unique way.
In newspapers and magazines, they published recipes and articles and encouraged women to be budget holders and respond to the challenge of turning carrot-cream spaghetti-likes into tasty dishes.
A Square Meal is a celebration of historical treats.
What is particularly tasty are the state leadership's responses to its hungry citizens, such as President Herbert Hoover's "let me eat cake" attitude.
At the same time as he publicly praised the simple food of his childhood on an Iowa farm, he himself ate, like a millionaire and a refined gentleman who he had become.
"He liked continental European cuisine, such as fish and cucumber sauce," says Coe.
"He ate in the almost gold-paved dining room, wearing a tuxedo.
“He was completely estranged from reality.”
President Roosevelt might not have made the same mistake if his wife Eleanor hadn't encouraged him to set an example on his stomach.
As stated in the book, when Mrs. Roosevelt hired a housekeeper who was not at all interested in taste, the dishes served in the White House were not only the most boring in Washington, but also the worst-prepared."
Many recessionary foods were abandoned as soon as the country got back on its feet.
According to Jane Ziegelman, the continuation of the tradition is still reflected in how we pay attention to calories and food intake and how science has been applied to cooking.
As the couple wrote the book, they began to better understand Ziegelman's mother's eating habits, such as why she couldn't throw food away.
"He was really afraid that food would have to be thrown away," says Coe.
"People even began to feel bad about his food," recalls Ziegelman.
"For example, once for wind hats."
When the main food plates had been emptied in Eisenberg's lunch bar, Ziegelman and Coe pulled out the plum dessert they had prepared at home, the same one that President Roosevelt ate for his lunch.
"It's called plum foam," says Ziegelman and adds that giving a flashy name to modest recipes was a common trick.
There was no whipped cream, only plums, a little flour, sugar, water and cinnamon.
We reluctantly dipped our spoons into thick brown goo.
Surprisingly, it didn't taste bad.
Coe santed for the second time and the third.
"Walnuts would make this better," Coe says to his wife, sounding like a positive budget keeper.
Gilead's full compensation for attorney's fees for Pathetic Drug C patent dispute with Merck
The judge of the Regional Court of the United States ruled that Gilead Sciences Inc is entitled to receive full compensation from the attorneys' fees incurred for the patent dispute over hepatitis C drugs with the pharmaceutical manufacturer Merck & Co Inc.
Gilead was acquitted in June of US$200 million in damages it had been deserked for violating two patents owned by Merck in relation to Gilead's Sovaldi and Harvoni. A U.S. judiciary found that Merck had committed violations, which included impression and other unethical practices.
In Thursday's lawsuit, U.S. District Court Judge Beth Labson Freeman said Gilead was entitled to be exempted from attorneys-at-law fees inflicting its defense of the case.
Merck is trying to reach Gilead, which dominates the market with the next generation of hepatitis C drugs, which can be used to treat more than 90 percent of hepatitis C patients.
The case dates back to 2013, when Gilead and Merck sued each other and demanded the private ownership right of sofosbuvir under laboratory. Sofosbuvir is the active substance in Gilead's medicines.
U.S.-backed forces have seized the city of Manbiji from ISIS
U.S.-backed forces have seized control of Manjib in northern Syria near the Turkish border, after the last ISIS fighters left the city, a spokesman said.
The Syrian Democratic Forces (SDF) faction is now raking the city to find the last ISIS fighters hiding in the city.
Sharfan Darwish, a spokesman for the SDF's Military Council, told Reuters that 2 000 hostages kidnapped by ISIS fighters have been released.
"The city is now in full control, but we're still conducting search operations," Darwish said.
The Clintons earned US$10.75 million in 2015 and paid 34.2 percent federal tax
Democrat presidential nominee Hillary Clinton released her 2015 tax return on Friday, revealing that her and her husband's income was US$10.75 million at the time and their actual federal tax rate of 34.2.
In 2015, the Clintons donated one million dollars to charities, mostly the Clinton Foundation. Former President Bill Clinton made nearly US$5.3-million in Speech Remunerations, and Hillary Clinton, who previously served as Secretary of State, announced that she had earned 3 million dollars from Simon & Schuster for a book on her mandate at the State Department.
Clinton's vice-presidential nominee, Virginia Senator Tim Kaine and his wive Anne Holton announced 10-year tax returns.
Those the actual federal tax rate they paid in 2015 was 20.3.
"Hillary Clinton and Tim Kaine will continue to lead the way in terms of financial transparency," Jennifer Palmieri, Clinton's campaign aider, said in a statement.
The complete contrast is Donald Trump, who hides behind excuses and withdraws his promises to publish his tax records.
According to custom, U.S. presidential candidates have always published their tax records, even though they are not required by law.
New York businessman Trump and his lawyer have appealed to an audit by the tax government, saying it's the reason Trump hasn't disclosed his income data.
"Transfection is yours" tweeted Clinton's campaign aider Ian Sam, referring to the Democratic nominee's tax records.
On Thursday, Trump's Special Legal Adviser Michael Cohen told CNN he wouldn't let Trump release his tax information until the checks were completed.
Trump critics, including 2012 Republican presidential nominee Mitt Romney and other Republican members, have said his refusition to make public the information raises questions about Trump's own capital, his contributions to charity, business relations, and many other ties, including relations with Russia.
Clinton has tackled this issue and posted an online video on Friday in which she highlights prominent Republicans pushing Trump to release her tax records.
On Thursday, Clinton raised the issue in her economic speech in Michigan.
"Trump refuses to do what every presidential candidate has done for decades, and not to make their tax returns," Clinton told the crowd.
According to Politico, Trump did not pay taxes at all or paid little for two years in the 1990s, and a New York Times business columner quoted several tax lawyers and auditors on Friday that the situation could still be the same.
Federal tax rates have emerged as a key issue in the presidential elections.
Clinton has endorsed a rule named after billionaire investor Warren Buffett that would ensure those earning more than one million dollars a year would pay at least 30 percent tax.
President Barack Obama also endorses the proposal.
Last week in Omaha, Nebraska, a Clinton electoral rally, Buffett, whose Berkishire Hathaway commodiative headquarters is headquartered in the same location, challenged Trump to a meeting where they would both disclose their tax records.
According to Buffet, he has also been subjected to an audit by the Tax Administration and that Trump is not afraid of the tax authority, but of voters.
The Clinton campaign has published tax returns until 2007.
The Clintons, who now live in Chappaqua, State of New York, paid a real federal tax of an average of 32 percent between 2007 and 2014, and the actual combined tax rate they paid was about 40.5 percent.
It is clear from the Clincino's 2015 tax return that, unlike the majority of Americans, only 100 dollars of their income had wage earnings.
Their main investment was a low-cost index investment fund, and the Clinnians reported a sum of US$190,000 in dividend and interest income.
Kevin Drawbaugh, editor-in-chief Jonathan Oatis
Chili-filled firecracker condoms to support the protection of elephants in Tanzania
Conservationists in Tanzania have come up with an unusual way to keep elephants away from human habitation – elephants are rushed by throwing condoms stuffed with chili powder towards them.
The way has proven to be effective, and the Honeyguide Foundation, which came up with the idea a few years ago, has jointly launched a campaign aimed at teaching volunteers in the villages of Northern Tanzania how residents can protect their homes and crops without harming elephants.
Until some time ago, spears were used to shook elephants.
Friday is World Elephant Day dedicated to the protection of animals.
"After developing the method, we've seen attitudes changed in these villages, as now villagers can be confident they'll be able to keep the elephants out of their fields without damaging them," Damian Bell, executive director of the Honeyguide Foundation, said in a statement.
At first, the intrusive elephant is disturbed by pointing a flashlight and playing the horn.
If this doesn't work, let's throw a so-called chili cloud.
The chili powder mixed with the soil is put into the condom together with the firecracker, after which the end of the condom is twisted so that only the functition is left outside.
When the fusing wire is lit, the condom explodes open and spreads a fine cloud of chili powder into the air.
As a rule, one spice cloud is enough to drive the elephant away.
The last resort is a thrower, which shoots fireworks known as a Roman candle into the air and generates a loud and bright explosion.
Jewish film director criticizes Berlinian hotel for removing Israel's area code for "prompting Arabs" – RT News
A French film director at his roots gave birth to a medial rage after revealing that Berlin's Kempinski Bristol Hotel does not mention Israel's area code in its list of country numbers. The hotel employee pleaded when asked about "prompts".
Claude Lanzmann, author of the Holocaust's Shoah documentary, vented his frustration towards the Kempinski Bristol Hotel in Berlin by writing an open letter to the Frankfurter Allgemeine Zeitung (FAZ).
On his recent visit, Lanzmann tried to find Israel on the hotel's list of area codes.
However, it was not mentioned among the countries where you can call directly from the room.
"How is it possible that in 2016 in Berlin, in the capital of the new Germany, Israel has been completely destroyed – as if it didn't exist?"
Lanzmann writes.
Kempinski Bristol, on the other hand, offers the opportunity to call Israel through a separate call center.
The film director asked for an explanation of the hotel's reception.
Lanzmann writes that the receptionist's response shocked him.
The receptionist said Israel's removal from the list was "a decision carefully considered by the Kempinski hotel management."
The reason behind the procedure shocked the film director even more.
According to Lanzmann, the clerk said: "Most of our guests are Arabs, and they demand the removal of Israel's area code."
Interviewed by Suddeutsche Zeitung, Israel's ambassador to Germany, Yakov Hadas-Handelsman, called the incident "a great shame."
"The incident shocks and shocks us all.
It's a great shame.
And it's all the more disgraceful when we take into account that we're in Germany and it's exactly this hotel chain.
No further explanation is needed.
We expect the hotel to draw the right conclusions," says the Ambassador.
The incident caused quite a reaction on the network, where it was called a scandal.
Some accused the Kempinski Hotel of simply giving in to the alleged demands of Arabic customers.
"Shame" was said in a publication that highlighted the location of the hotel near the synagogue on Kurfurstendamm Street.
Der Spiegel writes that the hotel quickly denied the charges, called it a mistake and apologized to Lanzmann.
"A direct order to exclude Israel from the list of area codes was never given," writes Suddeutsche Zeitung Kempinski, a spokesman.
It adds that the area code has since been added to the list.
Police used tear gas and rubber bullets at a protest by Brazilian students
Students clashed with police in Sao Paulo on Thursday when they clashed to protest against the reforms of the government's public education system.
Police used tear gas and rubber bullets against demonstrators.
Rioting began when law enforcement authorities tried to disperse a group of students, as a result of which the police used rubber bullets and tears.
Hundreds of students who wanted to express their dismay with the government's plans took part in the demonstration.
Geraldo Alckmin, the governor of Sao Paulo, has proposed an austerity measure to close nearly a hundred public schools and place 300 000 students in new schools.
Some of the protesters made graffiti on the walls, temporarily protesting Michel Temer.
The student demonstration took place just a few days after the streets were filled with pro-demonstrators supporting President Dilma Rousseff when the Senate voted on the impeach.
Trade between Russia and Iran has increased by 71% – RT Business
Since the removal of the sanctions, the annual trade between Moscow and Tehran has increased by 70.9%, according to Russia's ambassador to Iran, Levan Dzhagaryan.
According to him, the interest of Russian companies in doing business in Iran has increased since international sanctions against Iran were lifted.
"Russian companies will continue existing relations with Iran, while new companies are ready to enter the market.
Of course, increasing trade will be one of the priorities for cooperation between the two countries," says Dzhagaryan.
He notes that in the first five months of 2016, trade between Russia and Iran increased by 70.9% compared to the same period in 2015 and rose to 856 million dollars.
"Especially Russia's exports to Iran quickly rose 91.5 percent, to 697 million dollars.
Russia's imports from Iran increased by 16 percent to US$158 million.
The deal was boosted by the sales of machinery, ground vehicles and weapons," says the ambassador.
Dzhagaryan also expects that a record number of Iranian tourists will travel to Russia this year.
"Last year, Russia's foreign mission in Iran granted Iranian citizens about 35 000 visas, most of which were travel visas.
In addition, for the first time, Iran was among the 20 first countries in terms of tourists, whose citizens travelled to Russia.
And it's not too daring to say that a record number of Iranian tourists travelling to Russia will break this year," he says.
The number of Russian tourists in Iran, on the other hand, is relatively small, which the ambassador hopes will change.
"I can personally recommend traveling to Isfahan, Shiraz and Yazdi cities, which have retained the monuments of ancient Persian civilization in good condition," he says.
Scotti Callum Skinner swung to the finish line as the Britons won their cycling gold
Scotland's Callum Skinner gained fame in the men's team sprint on Rio's velodrome last night, when the UK trio defended the 2012 title in London.
The 23-year-old Skinner from Glasgow played the role of an anchor, which he inherited from compatriot Sir Chris Hoy from his compatriot. The young man made the legend proud to glide the goal and snatched the victory from the pre-favorite New Zealand team.
Britain took a surprise victory four years ago when Hoy won his fifth of his six Olympic gold medals.
And they repeated the stunt after finishing only sixth in March at the World Championships in London, despite the fact that Hoy had since left his racing career.
The problem between Beijing and London was the replacement of Jamie Staff, who specialises in the role of the first leader.
Hindes came out at just the right time.
Hoy's decision to end his racing career also left a gap that Kian Emadi and Matt Crampton tried to fill.
Skinner from Scotland was a man that British coaches believed in and in which they saw the potential that he has now redeemed.
Hindes, Kenny and Skinner, who won the championship with Hoy four years ago, set an Olympic record in qualifying, but in the first round New Zealand was faster.
And Britain's trio won gold in a duel.
Hindes narrowly lagged behind after his round, but Kenny struggled for the team's lead, which was retained by Skinner.
A big part of the change was changing the strategy so that Hindes drove to slow down in a higher gear, which allowed Kenny and Skinner in particular to keep the pace.
Hoy watched from his stance as a commentator on the BBC and reached Skinner, Kenny and Hindes as they stepped onto the podium.
23-year-old Skinner from Glasgow says: "I'm flattered by the juxtaposition.
I'm just trying to do my best and succeed.
Were setting an Olympic record in the final and defeated world champions.
That's unbelievable.
“There’s no better way to
After defeating Kenny and Hoy in London in 2012, Hindes, together with Kenny and Hoy, deliberately crashed the track due to a bad start.
He later withdrew his words, and Britain's rivals had no remedy.
This time there were no such problems with a sovereign victory.
Hindes says: "The atmosphere was like in London, all British flags were flying in the stands.
Our team became unified in the last three months.
“We’ve lost so many world championships that it feels great to win the Olympics.”
In other words, one race, one gold medal for a country that won seven out of ten track championships both in Beijing and London.
If this is a metric for the progress made over the past five months, a successful five-day velodrome will lie ahead.
As Kenny has shown with his abilities, it is not impossible to imagine that he will replicate Hoy's achievements and win three Olympic gold medals in one Olympics.
This would mean accumulating the account with six gold medals and one silver medal for Kenny, who is exactly 12 years younger than Hoy and was also born on March 23 (the magical date of British sports, which is also the birthday of Steve Redgrave, Mo Farah and Roger Bannister).
The medal achievements would then be the same as Hoy's.
Kenny will next compete in the sprint, which will start today and be decided in three days.
The 28-year-old Kenny from Bolton says, "At the moment, I can only rejoice in this.
The team competition is always the best.
You get to enjoy the victory together with your friends.
Winning alone is a bit lonely."
Hindes says, "If he wins two more gold medals, he'll have to get knighty."
This was the only medal competition of the day, but Britain received more good news.
Sir Bradley Wiggins, Ed Clancy, Steven Burke and Owain Doull were the fastest in a four-man team pursuit.
It was 3:51.943.
The world record set in London in 2012 and winning gold is 3:51.659.
The first round and the final (at 22:42) will be competed today on the second day of the competition, with Wiggins at the center of attention.
Yesterday, he had a dispute with the race officials before qualifying, when officials invited him to measure his length.
In a similar women's competition that continues and ends tomorrow, Laura Trott, Joanna Rowsell Shand, Elinor Barker and Milngavie's Katie Archibald reached a world record time of 4:13.260.
Great Britain's team can now rely on the fact that its mighty track cycling team will take responsibility for the middle stage of the Olympics and bring a number of medals to the country.
After some truly exceptional evidence of a leading position in Beijing and London, expectations before Rio have been underestimated.
There have been dramas in the background, the least of which is the resignation of technical director Shane Sutton in April in the midst of allegations related to sexism and bullying, and the end of the rival careers of major athletes such as Hoy and Victoria Pendelton.
On the first night, however, competitions on the velodrome show that Britain's already legendary reputation to rise to the top at the perfect moment will once again take place.
The future of Portland's smelling plant at stake due to the termination of the electricity contract
The future of an aluminum smelting plant in the state of Victoria, Portland, and thus the jobs of thousands of workers are at stake when a power company providing electricity for the smelting plant said that one of the plant's owners is about to terminate the supply contract and it is expected that all supply contracts will be terminated.
This is the result of Victoria's government's decision earlier this year not to extend the subsidies with which the factory will be kept operational.
The fixed, 20-year support will expire in November.
As many as 2000 jobs are in danger of disappearing from the Alcoan Portland factory.
Due to the abolitions, up to 2000 direct or indirect jobs may be at risk.
The snowball effect can continue to increase, as the smelter consumes about 10 percent of Victoria's total electrical production.
In 2014, Alcoa closed Point Henry's smelting plant, resulting in the Anglesea power plant being shut down last year.
The Alcoan Portland smelter loses money and is in danger of having to close its doors.
The measures correspond to the speculation that has been going on for years about the future of the factory, which was first caused by the strong dollar in Australia and recently the price of the factory's production.
AGL, which is supplying electricity to a large aluminium smelter from its Loy Yang A power plant in Latrobe Valley, says it expects that three other smelter owners will also terminate their contracts.
The factory's majority owner is a joint venture consisting of Alcoa and Alumina. Chinese CITIC and Japanese Marubeni Corp own minority shares in the factory.
AGL says it expects Portland's production facility to remain operational, which means that the physical demand for electricity from the smelter will continue.
AGL says that the agreements are so-called hedging agreements, which were to enter into force in November 2016.
The notice period for the contracts is 12 months, so they will end in 2017.
"Thus, AGL expects contracts to generate revenue in fiscal year 2017," says the power plant.
Potential return effects on AGL from the financial year 2018 onwards are currently limited by strong future market prospects for wholesale electricity market prices.
AGL says it has already reduced the value of contracts in its bookkeeping with 187 million dollars "as a prediction for the termination of contracts."
According to Alcoa, the decision to terminate the contracts "won't affect Alcoa's ... capabilities to keep the smelting plant in operation or secure electricity supplies to the smelter."
The U.S. company said it had signed a 20-year deal with Loy Yang in 2010, which was due to come into effect from November this year, although it still needs to figure out what it has now withdrawn.
Due to the poor economic condition of the industry, analysts have been cautious about their assessments of both a smelting plant operating in the state of Victoria, Portland, and the Tomago factory near Newcastle.
Earlier this week, Stephen Mikkelsen, AGL's Chief Operating Officer, told analysts in a speech that Portland uses approximately 600 megawatts of electricity and Tomago about 900 megawatts.
"If these factories exit the market, it will mean a significant decrease in demand in the (electricity) market.
As a producer, it wouldn't be good news," says Mikkelsen.
Kardashianit reality TV star Kendall on September's Vogue's cover
Kardashian-Jenner clan's fourth daughter, Kendall Jenner, 20, poses in Vogue's famous September issue.
Kendall, who is Estee Lauder's advertising face and performed at Milan, Paris and New York Fashion Weeks, announced the news on his Instagram account on Thursday.
‘Looking the deck in a room full of my loved ones brought me to tears,’ Kendall wrote.
The release of the cover was followed by a bizary (or perhaps just a Kardashian series style?), a script-written video featuring all members of the Kardashian-Jenner family (the bar troublemakers Rob and Caitly's and everyone's favorite uncle, Ryan Seacrest of the Kardashians program), pretending to have forgotten about his sister's cover.
The plot is clichéd and is used in almost all birthday episodes of stepshows (Seacrest utters the words: "Did you think we had forgotten you?" as sincerely as the children's morning TV cartoon character, but Krisi had to get the rest of his daughters involved in this announcement in some way.
Having appeared in Vogue's September issue unquestionably signifies a new Kardashian era.
Kendall's older sister, Kim, may have gotten into Vogue's lawsuit before (she appeared on Vogue's cover with her husband Kanye West in April 2014), but the magazine's September issue is in a class of its own.
The September issue is Vogue's best-selling release a year.
The issue, which resembles a large picture book in terms of thickness, attracts a large number of advertisers (the fashion website Fashionista calculated that last year's 832-page book had 615 pages of advertisements).
The publication of the cover star of the September issue is so eagerly awaited that in the previous two years the newspaper's images have leaked to the public even before the official announcement.
Earlier, September's cover models have been Naomi Campbell, Linda Evangelista and Kate Moss.
In recent years there have been celebrities who work for the model rather than models, which are celebrities.
Oscar-winner and Dior-advertising Jennifer Lawrence was on the cover in 2013, and a year later Lady Gaga was honored.
The magazine describes Jenner as a successful model of her generation, and even if she is more of a model-working celebrity, she is still in pretty good company.
These celebrities aren't relatives of well-known people, reality TV stars, or contemporary children who know how to make a good Snapchat video (although Jenner's all of these).
You can rightly call them talented women who succeed in the entertainment industry above all others.
Beyonce, who appeared on the cover last year, has one of the strongest years of his career when he released his Lemonade album.
Of course, not everyone is happy about Jenner's success.
Due to his reality TV background, Jenner's position in the fashion world will always be awkward.
In June, Victoria's Secret's original model Stephanie Seymour criticized Jenner and his colleague Gigi Hadid by calling them "bitches."
These feelings have swirled to the surface again when the news of Jenner's appearance on Vogue's cover came out.
However, the constant stalking of dissatisfaction seems irrelevant.
Kendall Jenner is Vogue's September issue cover model.
He's a supermodel, in the purest sense of the word.
She's walking at Marc Jacobs fashion shows.
He's the advertising face of Estee Lauder foundations.
She and all her sisters sell pictures of themselves through paid apps.
He sells pictures of Cindy Crawford's daughter, whom he photographed for LOVE magazine.
And in her spare time, she performs on reality TV, perhaps just as a way to meet her sister and her children.
Legal aid centres turn away 160 000 people a year
More than 160 000 of the country's most vulnerable people are turned away from legal aid centres each year, and the sector is preparing for the effects that additional budget cuts will have on centres that are already at the limit.
In Queensland's state, three of those seeking free legal aid and advocacy services are already being turned away from five people, most of them elderly people, immigrants, vulnerable workers, victims of violence and unjustified eviction and similar injustices without resources.
Public legal aid services, the workload of which is already overwhelmed, are preparing for further cuts in appropriations.
Starting June next year, legal aid centers are at risk of losing another 30 percent of federal funding.
James Farrell, director of Queensland's Public Legal Aid Centre, says that according to the latest calculation report compileded by the National Agency for Legal Aiding Centers, thousands of people are already being turned away from the centres each year.
If the federal government doesn't swirl its decision, Farrell says the situation will only get worse.
"Queensland's public legal aid centers are constantly underfunded, which means that thousands of people are turned away as they apply for the necessary services they need," says Farrell.
“People from violent relationships, people under uncontrollable and crippling debt, divorced families, unjustifiably dismissed workers – all of them have problems with legal matters, and this report shows that they can’t get the help they need.
The federal government plans to cut funding by 30% since June 2017, which means that thousands of the most vulnerable in Queensland will have to cope with complex legal problems without assistance.
We appeal to Chancellor of Justice and Queensland Senator George Brandes so that these funding cuts will be phased out immediately so that people can get the legal aid they need.
Last year, the state government fulfilled its promise to bring legal aid funding to the national intermediate level.
Now is the time to take into account the needs of public legal aid centers and their clienteles, and Palaszczuki's government must invest in this important work."
A lawsuit was filed against Donald Trump's campaign to threaten an employee with a gun
New York: A lawsuit was fileded against Donald Trump's campaign when no action was expected to be taken since its leadership was told that the campaign's North Carolina executive had threatened an employee with a gun while they were driving the Jeep in February.
The event will be filmed in a lawsuit fileded by Vincent Bordini, a former software educator of the state campaign on Wednesday in a state court in Charlotte.
Bordini says he waited months for the situation to be dealt with internally before, according to his own words, understood that this was not going to happen.
Bordini says in his lawsuit that campaign director Earl Phillip "took out the pistol, put his right index finger on the trigger, and pressed the barrel into Vincent's knocop."
According to the lawsuit, after filing a complaint to the local and regional management of the campaign, Bordini learned that at least four other people had experienced the same with Phillip.
"Even if you don't launch a gun, it's causing damage," says Bordini in his lawsuit.
The lawsuit comes just two days after the Republican presidential nominee caused public outrage when he told the crowd in North Carolina that "supporters of the second additional clause" – who is considered to be gun owners and arms enthusiasts – could take action if his opponent Hillary Clinton wins the election and is allowed to elect judges.
According to Trump's campaign personnel, the media detached his words from their context, and with his words Trump did not encourage violence.
In his lawsuit, Bordini demands compensation from the campaign for an indifferent management and Phillip's compensation for assault, assault and the emotional suffering caused.
Trump spokeswoman Hope Hicks did not immediately respond to a message asking for a comment.
Despite the attempts, Phillip could not be reached.
Charlotte's local news agency WCCB said Phillip recently fileded the campaign.
There was no immediate response to Phillip's consulting company, Innovative Consulting Services.
Republican presidential nominee Donald Trump has been engaged in a controversial campaign.
Bordini v. Donald J. Trump For President Inc., 16-CS-14300, General Court of Justice, Superior Court District County of Mecklenburg (Charlotte).
The UK must use the Olympic spirit to expand Heathrow
Heathrow also revitalizes the economy and creates well-being – and not only in local communities and London, but throughout the country.
For me, this was something that interested me the most at Heathrow, and as at the London Olympics in 2012, we invested enormously in ensuring that our entire nation and all our regions benefit from them.
The Olympics brought tremendous benefits to Stratford and the East End.
We are doing this again by creating opportunities for local people and businesses near Heathrow.
For local communities alone, we will create 10 000 new apprenticeships and 40 000 new jobs, which will eliminate youth unemployment in the neighbouring areas of the airport.
As with the 2012 London Olympics, there are always people who think the expansion of Heathrow is too much of a challenge.
With regard to the Olympics, I learned that Britain will never be ready for them and that we will find ourselves in an awkward situation in the eyes of the rest of the world.
The skeptics were mistaken in 2012, and they're going to be wrong about Heathrow as well.
There will always be obstacles to overcome, but Heathrow will answer every question you've been asked.
Like the Olympics, Heathrow is an infrastructure project that will benefit many, rather than few.
Expansion is an opportunity to create up to 180 000 new jobs and increase the economy by 211 billion pounds, bringing know-how and employment both to the airport's environment and to the United Kingdom as a whole.
At Heathrow, we have a great working group that has carried out on time and by remaining within the budget an 11-billion-pound infrastructure investment program, which included the T5 terminal – which has been voted the best air terminal of the year for two consecutive years – and the T2 terminal, which was built in the middle of two of the world’s most congested runways.
Now they are ready to produce the third runway, which is supported by a private investment of 16 billion pounds.
The London Olympics in 2012 were a great success for our country, and I am convinced that it will also be an extension of Heathrow.
It's time for us to use the same Olympic spirit to expand Heathrow to guarantee the future of our trade and to create know-how and employment that Britain can be proud of.
Lordy Paul Deighton is the chairman of Heathrow's board of directors and a former commercial secretary of the Treasury Department.
Adblock Plus and Facebook ad-anti-advertising cat-mouse-play
Attemptents by companies earninging their money from online advertising to mislead anti-advertising software has turned into cat-and-mouse games, says one company that produces blocking programs.
On Thursday, just two days after Facebook announced on its own page that it was blocking anti-advertisement programs, Adblock Plus published a blog post where it said that users can update the filters of their blocking software and thus again block social media ads.
On Tuesday, Facebook announced that it would change the ad entry codes of its desktop site so that it would be more difficult for anti-advertisement programs to identify which parts of the page are ads.
However, Adblock now says it has cleared the new codes.
Adblock spokeswoman Ben Williams wrote in a blog post that "such a balling between the open-source anti-adventure community and deceptors has been going on ever since ad-blocking was invented" and that it's possible that Facebook is about to make its own corrective again.
The fact that the world's largest social media company has participated in the battle is a strong indication of how much of a concern advertisers have become in the industry.
An estimated 200 million people worldwide use anti-advertisement programs, according to a study conducted by Pagefair and Adobe last year. The figure has nearly quadrupled since 2013.
Facebook is used every day by 1.03 million active users on mobile devices, and mobile advertising already accounts for 84% of Facebook's advertising revenue.
However, the company intends to investigate anti-advertisement programs in its desktop app, as the spokesman says mobile ad-blocking apps don't work on Facebook's mobile apps and Instagram.
The revenues from Desktop ads are still high enough, which is why Facebook considered the investment to be worthwhile.
On Tuesday, Facebook also said it would update the user settings' advertising tool so that users can more control how ads are targeted at them.
On Thursday, Facebook criticized Adblock's new filters, claiming that they are also blocking the appearance of some regular Facebook posts.
"This is not a good experience for users, and we are going to solve the problem," a Facebook spokesperson said in an e-mail statement.
"Anti-advertising programs are clumsy and ineffective, and that's why we've invested in the development of user settings such as advertising tools so that people can control the targeting of ads themselves."
The woman committed suicide by abandoning cars on a Swiss highway
The victim lay on the highway in the middle of the night.
Police ask for eyewitness help on the highway in Thursday's first-hour accident, in which a woman deliberately rescheduled under several cars.
The accident happened on the A1 highway in the canton of Vaud near Morges shortly after midnight.
The woman did her desperate deed by lying down on a dark road and was hit by several cars, Vaud police said in a statement.
The victim was a 49-year-old Swiss woman and was clearly aimed at committinging suicide, including a lorry.
The woman died at the scene.
According to the police, many motorists involved in the accident have already been interviewed, but some have yet to be identified. The police ask them or other witnesses to report the accident.
Gilles Hofstetter, a lawyer, told Le Tribune de Geneve that a criminal investigation has been launched, which is a logical stage in a situation like.
However, it remains to be seen whether charges will be filed against the motorists involved in the accident.
"Was it impossible to avoid a collision?
It'll be cleared up by the prosecutor," says Hofstetter.
Over the past five years there have been two similar incidents, on the basis of which motorists are not held responsible.
According to the newspaper, in both cases, a person was walking on a highway in the dark and the court ruled that motorists could not wait to run into a pedestrian on the highway in the middle of the night.
Last year, a police officer was acquitted of involuntary management after colliding with a man who was lying on an A1 highway near Paynerne in August 2014.
The court heard that the 22-year-old victim had consumed plenty of alcohol and passed out on the road.
The court ruled that the driving method of the police – which was responding to the distress call – was justified and the man lying on the road was ‘completely exceptional and unpredictable.’
Anyone who knows anything about an accident near Morges on Thursday is asked to call the police +358 21 644 83 46 or +358 21 644 44 44.
NASA climate model: Venus has been viable
According to estimates by NASA's new climate model, Venus may have been viable.
NASA's Goddard Institute for Space Studies (GISS) developed a model to study Venus' past with the same tools and algorithms as those used by climate scientists.
The purpose of the model was to find out whether the conditions in Venus could sometimes have been similar to on Earth, even though Venus is hot and waterless and its atmosphere consists almost entirely of carbon dioxide.
According to previous presentations by the researchers, Venus's slow speed of rotation is explained by its thick atmosphere.
However, based on the latest simulation, it can be assumed that Venus has had a thin atmosphere corresponding to the Earth, and it has nevertheless rotated slowly.
The model was also used to determine the topography of early Venus.
There were likely to be more dry areas on the planet than on Earth, but there may have been enough water and geographical variation to maintain life and prevent rapid evaporation.
The early Venus also benefited from the sun, which was 30 per cent dimmer.
"GISS's modelling is rolling slowly, and it'll be daily for almost two months at a time per sun," GISS researcher Anthony Del Genio said in a press release.
This warms the surface and generates rain, which creates a thick layer of cloud. The cloud layer acts like an umbrella and protects the surface for a large part of the heat of the sun.
As a result, the average climate temperatures are even a few degrees lower than Earth's current temperatures.
The model was used to create the story of Venus, which corresponds to the previous assumptions of planetary scientists about its development.
However, although Venus may have been habitable for several billion years, it has been exposed to excessive heat and ultraviolet light.
Its oceans evaporated, and the ultraviolet light burned the vaporized water.
As the hydrogen escaped into space, all that was left was a thick carbon dioxide-rich atmosphere and a deadly greenhouse effect – i.e. Venus as it is even today.
Investigators reported the results of the new Venus climate modelling in the journal Geophysical Research Letters.
Patrick Roy relinquishes his position as Avalanche's coach
Patrick Roy resigned on Thursday as coach of the Colorado Avalanche and vice president of the club's ice hockey operations, citing a lack of influence in the team's decision-making.
Colorado's vice president/director, Joe Sakic, confirmed the news and announced that the club will immediately start looking for a new coach.
"For the past three years, I've been doing my job as head coach of the Colorado Avalanche team and vice president of the club's ice hockey operations vigorously, passionately and purposefully," says 50-year-old Roy in his statement.
"During the summer, I have been thinkinging for a long and hard time how I would be able to heal the team, to give it the necessary depth and raise it to a higher level.
In order for this to be achieved, the coach and the vice president of the club's hockey operations must be fully aligned with the organization.
He must also have a say in decisions that affect the performance of the team.
These conditions are not met at the moment."
Sakic, who hired Roy on May 23, 2013, thanked Roy for his contribution to the team.
"Patrick told me about his decision today," Sakic says.
"We are grateful for everything he has done for our organization, and we wish him all the best for the future."
Roy was awarded the Jack Adams Award as the league's best coach of the year of his first season as head coach of the Avalanche from 2013 to 2014.
Under his leadership, the team reached 112 points and won the Central Division Division, but Colorado lost to Minnesota in the playoffs and haven't made it to the next two seasons.
Avalanche had 39-39-4 points in 2015–2016 and went five points behind the Minnesota Wild when they played their second Wild Card spot in the Western Conference.
Hall of Fame goaltender Roy won four Stanley Cups in his career – two for the Montreal Canadiens and two for the Avalanche.
North Sea oil workers begin negotiations and halt the strike
Chief negotiators say that the wage strikes of oil workers in the North Sea will be interrupted when all the parties to the dispute participate in a new round of negotiations.
Strikes have been carried out against Wood Group, a company that provides oil field services, with drilling rigs located in the North Sea, which are under the control of the Royal Dutch Shell.
According to a joint statement by the parties, "the Wood Group and the trade unions Unite and RMT confirmed that they have agreed to restart the process in order to resolve the dispute."
From the beginning of next week, we will hold a number of full-day meetings in the next few weeks, where we will discuss the problems from fresh considerations.
Trade unions justified their recent activities, which, according to them, were the first in more than 25 years, in protest against possible wage cuts of up to 30 per cent.
Due to negative market pressures, Wood Group will cut about 9 per cent of the tariffs of its contractors operating in the North Sea this year.
The company's reduction in tariffs will comply with a 10% reduction in the prices of contractors working in countries in 20114.
According to the company, further cuts will give it room for manoeuvre, which is necessary in order to be able to cope with the downturn in the market.
Shell, on the other hand, has eliminated thousands of employees and announced its plans to exclude oil and gas drilling operations in up to 10 countries, in order to streamline its operations with the British energy company BG Group.
Lower crude oil prices and the economic situation, as a result of the UK's decision to leave the European Union in June, create pressure on energy companies.
Dave Anderson, a Member of the Scottish Parliament, met with Labour leaders earlier this week to discuss one of the biggest conflictions in the North Sea that has been experienced for an entire generation.
According to him, the North Sea is vital to the region's economy.
"In the North Sea and across the country, we need an economy that benefits many and not just the few," says Anderson.
Nearly US$1.4 billion is expected to be spent on new projects in the North Sea in 2016, compared with approximately US$7 billion in the last five years.
Kepler measures stellar rotating rate in the Pleiades
Kepler Space Telescope's most recent task has been to measure stellar rotation speed, which has helped astronomers to compile the most comprehensive list of stellar rotation speeds among Plejad stars to date.
Based on new data, astronomists will be able to find out why stars belonging to the same star cluster rotate at different speeds and how orbital times are associated with planetary formation.
"We hope that by comparing the results to other star clusters, we will learn more about the relationship between star weight and age and even the history of its solar system," says Luisa Rebull, a researcher at the Center for Infrared Processing and Analysis at the University of Calsaden in Pasadena.
Pleiades star cluster is located 445 light-years away from the Earth.
Many of the stars were born about 125 million years ago, which means they are about to reach their adult life.
They're running faster than ever right now.
When the speed is at its peak, large amounts of stellar wind erupt from growing stars.
These winds have a star-braking effect as they progress through the magnetic fields of the stars.
As the star ages, its speed of rotation begins to slow down.
Kepler measures the speed of rotation of each star by tracking the time between the appearance of recognisable star spots (which correspond to sunspots).
The latest catalogued rotation speed data suggests that larger stars rotate more slowly than smaller ones.
Rebull compares stars to dancers – the bigger ones swirl slower than the smaller ones.
“We’re seeing Plejads ‘dancing’ that slow swirls are typically larger, while the fastest swirls are typically very light stars,” says Rebull.
Because the Pleiades star cluster is so close, it acts as an ideal space laboratory, which allows the properties of stars to be compared in relation to rotational speeds.
"The Pleiadian stellation provides a mainstay for the theoretical models that handle the rotation of both younger and older stars," says Rebull.
We want to learn a lot more about how, when and where the stars are slowing down their rotational rate and stop their "dance".
Rebull and his colleagues present their most recent analysis of Pleiade's rotating speeds in their three new articles, which will soon be published in the Astronomical Journal.
